import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t,P as n,q as o}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var r=t("<h1>Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.7</h1> <h2>Shared rules for legitimacy, jurisdiction, sovereignty, coordination, and cross-framework power</h2> <p><strong>Tier:</strong> 1 — Constitutional Foundation<br> <strong>Status:</strong> Controlled disclosure-dependent observation successor<br> <strong>Revision:</strong> 0.2.7 (2026-08-21)<br> <strong>Responsible:</strong> Global Governance Frameworks initiative<br> <strong>Source freeze:</strong> Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.6 SHA-256 <code>09274187d73d495fe86dc1b8b257cf1b7f6e8ec6f7eee09b61250b7fcbbc0af6</code></p> <h3>Version history</h3> <ul><li><strong>0.2.7 (2026-08-21):</strong> Disclosure-dependent observation patch, generalizing a rule found implemented in a single framework during the crime-causation coverage review. Adds §9.8, which establishes that where an observation reaches a decision-maker only because a person chose to report, disclose, present, enrol, complain, or seek help, the channel measures the underlying condition and the willingness to enter the channel at the same time, and cannot separate them. An intervention that changes that willingness therefore changes the measurement independently of the condition, and may move the indicator opposite to the truth: restoring trust in a complaint route raises recorded complaints, and making a service feel unsafe lowers recorded need. §9.8 requires the Observation Contract for any consequential decision relying on such a channel to record the disclosure act and who must perform it, what the discloser risks by disclosing, the expected direction of the intervention’s effect on disclosure, and an independent channel not depending on the same act or a statement that none exists; where none exists, movement in the indicator is a prompt for inquiry and may not be recorded as an outcome. §9.8 further prohibits targets for, or funding, authorization, stage-progression, or continuation conditioned on, a reduction in recorded reports, complaints, incidents, claims, or requests for help, and prohibits treating a fall in recorded harm as evidence of reduced harm where the responsible institution also controls access to the reporting route. It establishes affirmatively that rising recorded disclosure in a channel whose accessibility has just improved is expected and is not by itself an adverse finding about the institution or the population. §9.8 is distinct from §11.4: self-validation concerns a control’s mechanical effect on its own trigger, whereas §9.8 concerns the population’s willingness to be observed at all. Extends §9.2 and Appendix C with two disclosure fields and §19 question 5 with a disclosure clause. This revision is additive and restrictive and does not alter the v0.2.6 authority, jurisdiction, consequence, response, clock, lifecycle, information-class, person-continuity, scope-condition, institutional-lifecycle, valuation-record, dependency-completeness, or automatic-control semantics. No observation, evaluation, reporting, or measurement authority is created, and no framework acquires jurisdiction over another’s channel by recording its disclosure dependence. GMEAIA remains the sole operational source for handoff, response, omission, unresolved-duty, and closure states. Frameworks explicitly pinned to <code>CIS/0.2.6</code> or earlier remain valid against those declared compatibility targets; conformance to v0.2.7 attaches when they next substantially revise or expressly adopt it.</li> <li><strong>0.2.6 (2026-08-10):</strong> Valuation-record and dependency-completeness patch following the financial-function authority audit of the economic cluster. Adds <strong>Appendix N</strong>, a constitutional minimum for any valuation on which a consequential decision depends, recording valuation purpose, valuing authority and its independence from the decision, method and input provenance, as-of date and validity horizon, liquidity assumption, market-observability status, uncertainty and sensitivity, known divergence from other valuations of the same object, prohibited reuse, and challenge, correction and supersession routes. N.1 prohibits reuse outside the stated purpose without separate authority and a new record, treatment of a valuation as the value of an object rather than its value for a stated purpose, presentation of modelled or non-market valuations as observed prices, omission of uncertainty on the ground that a decision requires a single figure, adoption of an external rating or index without recording its method status and conflicts, and conversion between market price and care, ecological, cultural, sacred, or relational recognition where a protected non-convertibility applies. N.2 establishes that two conforming valuations of the same object for different purposes may differ, that the difference is not by itself an error, and that material divergence shall remain visible to the deciding party rather than be reconciled into a single figure. N.3 applies the Appendix L proportionality rule. Adds §19 question 22, anchored to the assumed operating conditions recorded under §2.15 and Appendix L.2: each such condition is traced to the external function, institution, infrastructure, or state variable it depends on; that function is classified as allocated to an identified owner, disclaimed as out of scope, or unnamed; and any unnamed function whose effect the framework’s own machinery amplifies, or which converts an outcome of the framework into an input to itself, is resolved as a deliberate non-goal, a seam requiring an interface, an observation gap, or a capability no identified actor is required or able to supply. Adds non-conformance category <strong>P3 Dependency gap</strong> for a function that materially determines outcomes and is amplified by the framework’s operation while being neither allocated nor disclaimed — a class in which no interface is malformed because none is represented, and which conformance review of existing interfaces therefore cannot detect. Extends question 5 with a valuation clause. The motivating audit finding was that where the corpus named an economic function it allocated that function correctly, and its failures were confined to functions it had never named at all. This revision is additive and restrictive and does not alter the v0.2.5 authority, jurisdiction, consequence, response, clock, lifecycle, information-class, person-continuity, scope-condition, institutional-lifecycle, or automatic-control semantics. No valuation authority, accounting standard, rating function, canonical value, or valuation-review body is created; valuation authority remains with the competent constituting instrument, and no framework acquires jurisdiction over another’s valuation by recording a divergence from it. GMEAIA remains the sole operational source for handoff, response, omission, unresolved-duty, and closure states. Frameworks explicitly pinned to <code>CIS/0.2.4</code> or <code>CIS/0.2.5</code> or earlier remain valid against those declared compatibility targets; conformance to v0.2.6 attaches when they next substantially revise or expressly adopt it; an additive patch that alters no conformance-relevant semantics does not by itself attach it.</li> <li><strong>0.2.5 (2026-08-08):</strong> Scope-condition and institutional-lifecycle patch following the Governance as Engineering conformance audit of the limits-of-perception cluster (Papers XVI–XXVII). Extends §2.15 so that frameworks state the conditions under which their mechanisms are expected to function and the conditions under which they are not, and adds <strong>Assumed operating conditions</strong> and <strong>Stated non-applicability</strong> to the Appendix L.2 falsification minimum; the existing §2.15 requirement to state narrowing, suspension, and replacement conditions is unchanged, and the audit found it substantially unimplemented across the corpus rather than absent from this Specification. Adds §16.1.1, which requires each institution to be recorded as maintaining either a finite task or a standing condition; requires finite-task institutions to state a completion criterion and prohibits remit expansion whose effect is to defer it; prohibits sunset clauses on standing-condition institutions and makes transfer rather than lapse their terminal act; establishes that where effective performance appears as the absence of an event, that absence is not evidence of obsolete function, and that dissolution, merger, or material defunding of such an institution requires affirmative demonstration that the underlying condition no longer obtains, not favourable readings on indicators the institution itself produces; and requires the §16.2 review to assess warning value and connective value as axes separate from mandate performance, on the finding that both are invisible to performance assessment and register only after the function is gone. The term <em>sentinel</em> in §9.7 retains its alerting sense and is not the warning role of §16.1.1. This revision is additive and restrictive and does not alter the v0.2.4 authority, jurisdiction, consequence, response, clock, lifecycle, information-class, person-continuity, or automatic-control semantics. No dissolution, funding, appointment, review-body, or institutional-classification authority is created; function type is recorded by the competent constituting instrument, and §16.2 continues to govern who may authorize. GMEAIA remains the sole operational source for handoff, response, omission, unresolved-duty, and closure states. Frameworks explicitly pinned to <code>CIS/0.2.4</code> or earlier remain valid against those declared compatibility targets; conformance to v0.2.5 attaches when they next revise or expressly adopt it.</li> <li><strong>0.2.4 (2026-08-08):</strong> Person-continuity and purpose-limited-attestation patch following the civil-registration/legal-identity ownership audit and the Kinship/Aurora boundary corrections. Adds a narrow constitutional interface for recognizing that purpose-limited records may concern the same person, correcting mistaken merges/splits/collisions, and ingesting externally constituted civil-status changes such as death without creating a civil registry or universal GGF identity. Establishes the doctrine <strong>recognition is not registration; linkage is not identity; attestation is not jurisdiction</strong>; makes same-person linkage non-transitive by default; prohibits universal correlation keys and silent cross-domain identity promotion; requires source-preserving correction rather than master-record rewriting; and preserves accrued claims, liabilities, remedies, succession questions, and unresolved duties across externally established death/status transitions. Adds Appendix M as a minimum Person-Continuity and Attestation Record. No birth/death registration, citizenship, nationality, migration-status, family-law, voting-eligibility, legal-name, legal-capacity, biometric-identity, universal-authentication, or proof-of-personhood authority is created. GMEAIA remains the sole operational source for handoff, response, omission, unresolved-duty, and closure states.</li> <li><strong>0.2.3 (2026-08-07):</strong> Controlled corrective successor to v0.2.2 following conformance review. Preserves the Recognition-Record Purpose Firewall and backward-compatibility doctrine introduced in v0.2.2 while removing weaker duplicate Love Ledger wording, routing disputed or cross-domain responsibility for creation of purpose-appropriate financial or operational records through the §18.1 conflict rule, and restoring section-break formatting. No broader authority, jurisdiction, consequence, response, clock, lifecycle, information-class, or automatic-control semantics changed. Frameworks explicitly pinned to <code>CIS/0.2.1</code> or <code>CIS/0.2.2</code> remain valid against those declared compatibility targets; new frameworks and substantive revisions should use v0.2.3 unless they identify and justify a different compatible target.</li> <li><strong>0.2.2 (2026-08-07):</strong> Recognition-record purpose firewall and compatibility patch. Generalized the Love Ledger boundary into a cross-framework rule for purpose-bound recognition records; anchored those records to the existing Class C and Class D information taxonomy; added constituting-purpose and secondary-use fields to Observation Contracts; prohibited silent conversion of care, ecological, civic, cultural, relational, and analogous contribution records into unrelated consequential signals; added a necessity and less-intrusive-source test for exceptional secondary use; required purpose-appropriate financial or operational records to be created when a recognized activity lawfully generates an asset, payment, entitlement, liability, or transaction; aligned the rule with the prohibition on generalized reputation enforcement; and added migration guidance. This revision is additive and restrictive and does not alter the v0.2.1 authority, jurisdiction, consequence, response, clock, or lifecycle semantics. Frameworks explicitly pinned to <code>CIS/0.2.1</code> remain valid against that declared compatibility target and are not required to repin solely because v0.2.2 exists; conformance to v0.2.2 attaches when they next revise or expressly adopt it. New frameworks and substantive revisions should use v0.2.2 unless they identify and justify a different compatible target.</li> <li><strong>0.2.1 (2026-08-05):</strong> Coherence patch. Preserved the v0.2 constitutional additions while resolving the consequence-profile namespace collision, separating capacity limitation from constitutional absence, clarifying the source and effect of response duties, making GMEAIA the sole operational source for controlled states and clocks, narrowing affected-party standing to participation, consent, challenge, and remedy, aligning jointly constituted authority, objective-reproduction review, falsification scope, and the current stack-migration sequence.</li> <li><strong>0.2 (2026-08-05):</strong> Recursive systems-audit integration following comparative review of Integrated Meta-Governance v1.5, Nested Economies v3.2, Institutional Regeneration v0.9.1, and Implementation & Adaptation v1.1.1. Added causal responsibility, duties attached to retained jurisdiction, response and omission accountability, constitutional-gap handling, proportionate documentation, common-mode dependency review, objective-reproduction analysis, and framework-level falsification. Extended the Subsidiarity Test and conformance questions without creating automatic authority transfer or a Meta-Governance residual competence.</li> <li><strong>Editorial edition (2026-08-02).</strong> No substantive change. Removed version pins from body prose so that a reference names an instrument rather than asserting a live dependency that goes stale when a framework moves; editions consulted are recorded here instead. Moved the front-matter source lists into this version history. Retitled Appendix I, which named a superseded Polycentric edition in its heading while §20.1 referred to the current one.</li> <li><strong>0.1 (2026-08-01):</strong> First edition. Primary source frameworks: Treaty for Our Only Home v1.2; Invitational Framework for Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing v1.0; Integrated Meta-Governance Framework v1.4; Polycentric Governance Architecture Framework v0.5. Compatibility frameworks: AUBI v2.5; Financial Systems v3.4; Nested Economies v3.2; Work in Liberation v2.0.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>Preamble</h1> <p>Humanity requires coordination across territories, ecosystems, economies, cultures, generations, and domains of expertise. It also requires protection against the recurring failure in which coordination becomes a claim to superior knowledge, superior legitimacy, or permanent command.</p> <p>This Specification defines the constitutional interfaces through which Global Governance Frameworks institutions may relate without collapsing their distinct sources of authority into one hierarchy.</p> <p>It begins from six observations:</p> <ol><li><strong>Legitimacy is plural.</strong> Indigenous peoples, persons, communities, democratic public authorities, treaty parties, rights-bearing ecosystems, professional communities, and shared-commons institutions do not derive all authority from one source.</li> <li><strong>Interdependence is real.</strong> Local autonomy cannot justify ungoverned harm to other peoples, territories, ecosystems, or shared commons.</li> <li><strong>Observation is limited.</strong> No indicator, model, dashboard, algorithm, council, or epistemic tradition possesses a complete view of the system it helps govern.</li> <li><strong>Coordination is not sovereignty.</strong> An institution that connects jurisdictions does not thereby acquire authority over them.</li> <li><strong>Continuity matters.</strong> Crisis action, veto, charter revocation, institutional transfer, or dissolution must stop harm without casually destroying rights, material security, essential services, or protected knowledge.</li> <li><strong>Responsibility can disappear between valid authorities.</strong> A constitution may constrain every actor correctly while leaving delay, refusal, incapacity, or a genuine jurisdictional gap without a named owner.</li> <li><strong>A person can become fragmented across valid systems.</strong> Purpose-limited identifiers and attestations may be necessary where civil documentation is absent, inaccessible, disputed, or unnecessary, but cross-domain recognition can itself become a surveillance or exclusion mechanism unless continuity, correction, and non-linkage rules are explicit.</li></ol> <p>The Specification therefore establishes a polycentric constitutional order. Territorial councils govern place-based public functions. Commons institutions govern cross-boundary ecological relations. Practice and labour institutions govern professional and economic standards within their mandate. Indigenous peoples exercise inherent sovereignty through their own authorities and laws. Treaty institutions exercise only authority validly delegated or constituted under the Treaty. Meta-Governance coordinates interfaces but possesses no free-standing substantive jurisdiction.</p> <p>The Polycentric Governance Architecture Framework contributes an important structural insight to this Specification: separation of powers must be horizontal as well as vertical. Territory, commons, economic practice, and Indigenous sovereignty create overlapping jurisdictions that can check one another. This Specification retains that topology while rejecting any claim that overlap makes capture impossible, that one GGF-designed Indigenous council can represent all Indigenous authority, or that automatic metrics, reputation systems, sortition juries, or economic sanctions may safely replace constitutional process.</p> <hr> <h1>1. Status, purpose, and scope</h1> <h2>1.1 Purpose</h2> <p>This Specification supplies shared constitutional rules for:</p> <ul><li>sources of legitimacy;</li> <li>jurisdiction and subsidiarity;</li> <li>Indigenous sovereignty and consent;</li> <li>polycentric separation of powers;</li> <li>cross-boundary commons;</li> <li>local adaptation, refusal, veto, and exit;</li> <li>observation and epistemic governance;</li> <li>automatic controls;</li> <li>deadlock and adjudication;</li> <li>emergency powers;</li> <li>institutional bypass, succession, and dissolution;</li> <li>protected material and informational continuity;</li> <li>purpose-limited person recognition, cross-domain continuity, and attestation boundaries;</li> <li>interfaces among GGF frameworks.</li></ul> <h2>1.2 Design status</h2> <p>This document is a <strong>design specification</strong>, not a self-executing treaty or source of public authority.</p> <p>Within the GGF framework family, later documents should conform to it unless they explicitly identify and justify a constitutional divergence. Public institutions acquire authority only through valid law, treaty, democratic mandate, Indigenous law, compact, charter, or other legitimate constituting process.</p> <h2>1.3 Normative language</h2> <ul><li><strong>Must / shall</strong> indicates a required design constraint.</li> <li><strong>Must not / shall not</strong> indicates a prohibited design.</li> <li><strong>Should</strong> indicates a strong presumption that may be departed from only with written justification.</li> <li><strong>May</strong> indicates a permitted option.</li></ul> <h2>1.4 Non-certification clause</h2> <p>This Specification can evaluate the internal coherence of GGF proposals concerning Indigenous sovereignty. It cannot certify that an institution, person, council, teaching, protocol, quotation, or interpretation is legitimate within any Indigenous people or nation.</p> <p>Substantive legitimacy requires Indigenous-determined processes. The GGF may bind itself to obligations of non-interference, consent, restitution, support, and respect. It may not manufacture the authority from which it seeks legitimacy.</p> <h2>1.5 Scope boundaries</h2> <p>This Specification does not fully define:</p> <ul><li>the substantive law of international crimes;</li> <li>the jurisdiction and procedure of the Digital Justice Tribunal;</li> <li>the scientific content of planetary boundaries;</li> <li>the internal law of any Indigenous people;</li> <li>Hearts, Leaves, reserves, or financial settlement;</li> <li>AUBI eligibility and payments;</li> <li>worker and provider standards;</li> <li>technical cybersecurity architecture;</li> <li>civil registration, including birth and death registration;</li> <li>legal name, parentage, adoption, marriage, divorce, guardianship status, and other substantive family/civil-status determinations;</li> <li>legal capacity, citizenship, nationality, migration status, residence status, voting eligibility, inheritance entitlement, or state-issued legal identity;</li> <li>a universal GGF identity, universal proof-of-personhood system, biometric identity infrastructure, or general authentication service.</li></ul> <p>Those belong to competent domain law, institutions, or frameworks. This Specification governs the interfaces and authority boundaries among them.</p> <hr> <h1>2. Constitutional commitments</h1> <p>All conforming GGF frameworks shall observe the following commitments.</p> <h2>2.1 Plural legitimacy</h2> <p>No single institution is presumed to originate all valid authority.</p> <p>The constitutional order shall recognize distinct legitimacy sources, including:</p> <ul><li>inherent Indigenous sovereignty;</li> <li>personal dignity and fundamental rights;</li> <li>democratic authorization;</li> <li>treaty consent;</li> <li>affected-community consent;</li> <li>delegated compact authority;</li> <li>fiduciary stewardship of shared commons;</li> <li>professional self-governance within rights and public-interest limits;</li> <li>lawful adjudicative authority.</li></ul> <p>Conflict among these sources requires a defined interface, not an assumed hierarchy.</p> <h2>2.2 Minimum necessary authority</h2> <p>A higher-scale, cross-boundary, or coordinating body may exercise only the least authority necessary to address a demonstrated:</p> <ul><li>cross-boundary externality;</li> <li>shared-commons obligation;</li> <li>fundamental-rights violation;</li> <li>treaty duty;</li> <li>jurisdictionally valid crisis;</li> <li>coordination problem that lower-scale bodies cannot adequately resolve with support.</li></ul> <p>Authority shall be limited in subject, territory, duration, and remedy. It shall return when the justification ends.</p> <h2>2.3 Bounded coordination</h2> <p>No body shall be designed on the assumption that it can observe or optimize the whole constitutional system.</p> <p>Every institution shall publish:</p> <ul><li>its jurisdiction;</li> <li>known blind spots;</li> <li>dependencies;</li> <li>excluded matters;</li> <li>uncertainty;</li> <li>challenge routes;</li> <li>conditions for devolution or dissolution.</li></ul> <h2>2.4 Non-creation of Indigenous sovereignty</h2> <p>Indigenous sovereignty is recognized, not delegated by the GGF.</p> <p>The GGF shall not:</p> <ul><li>decide who legitimately speaks for an Indigenous people;</li> <li>create a universal Indigenous council;</li> <li>define mandatory internal selection procedures;</li> <li>impose representation quotas as a substitute for affected-nation consent;</li> <li>compel disclosure of protected knowledge;</li> <li>make participation in GGF economic systems conditional on accepting a GGF-designed Indigenous institution.</li></ul> <h2>2.5 Decision-chain separation</h2> <p>Consequential action shall distinguish:</p> <ol><li>observation;</li> <li>interpretation;</li> <li>proposal;</li> <li>authorization;</li> <li>implementation;</li> <li>adjudication;</li> <li>outcome evaluation;</li> <li>reversal, restoration, or restitution.</li></ol> <p>No body should control the complete chain. A body that materially benefits from a diagnosis shall not be its sole interpreter or authorizer.</p> <h2>2.6 Indicators are not judgments</h2> <p>An institutional indicator, ecological threshold, crisis alert, model disagreement, public-trust measure, transaction pattern, variety-gap estimate, participation rate, or composite dashboard may justify attention or bounded containment.</p> <p>It does not by itself establish:</p> <ul><li>guilt;</li> <li>illegitimacy;</li> <li>capture;</li> <li>failure of sovereignty;</li> <li>authority transfer;</li> <li>sanction;</li> <li>dissolution;</li> <li>economic invalidation;</li> <li>suspension of rights.</li></ul> <h2>2.7 Protected continuity</h2> <p>Every crisis intervention, veto, charter revocation, institutional succession, or dissolution shall protect, to the greatest extent compatible with stopping the harm:</p> <ul><li>human rights;</li> <li>AUBI Layer 1;</li> <li>personal balances and valid claims;</li> <li>access to essential services;</li> <li>worker and participant rights;</li> <li>records needed for correction and appeal;</li> <li>Indigenous and community data control;</li> <li>ecological safeguards;</li> <li>restoration for erroneous action.</li></ul> <h2>2.8 Protected non-transfer</h2> <p>Interoperability shall support an explicit outcome of <strong>protected non-transfer</strong>.</p> <p>Knowledge, data, authority, or value may remain local, confidential, sacred, non-comparable, non-convertible, or available only through a bounded attestation. A right to audit institutional authority does not create a right to inspect all knowledge supporting that authority.</p> <h2>2.9 Refusal and non-participation</h2> <p>Individuals, communities, BAZs, states, and Indigenous peoples possess different forms of refusal and exit according to their legal and constitutional status.</p> <p>The framework shall distinguish:</p> <ul><li>refusal to participate;</li> <li>refusal to disclose;</li> <li>local adaptation;</li> <li>conscientious objection;</li> <li>jurisdictional objection;</li> <li>veto;</li> <li>withdrawal from a compact;</li> <li>withdrawal from a treaty;</li> <li>non-compliance with an unlawful act.</li></ul> <p>These shall not be collapsed into one category of resistance or system failure.</p> <h2>2.10 Reversible containment before irreversible consequence</h2> <p>Where immediate action is necessary under uncertainty, the default response shall be the narrowest reversible containment capable of preventing serious harm while preserving evidence and rights.</p> <p>Permanent sanction, authority transfer, dissolution, or economic invalidation requires accountable authorization and review.</p> <h2>2.11 Causal responsibility</h2> <p>Responsibility shall be allocated with regard to:</p> <ul><li>control over the principal causal variables;</li> <li>lawful jurisdiction;</li> <li>practical capacity;</li> <li>resources available to discharge the duty;</li> <li>cross-boundary and upstream effects.</li></ul> <p>Affected-party standing determines participation, evidence, challenge, consent where applicable, and remedy. It does not itself transfer responsibility to those experiencing the consequences.</p> <p>The place where consequences appear does not by itself determine where responsibility belongs. A lower-scale body shall not be treated as capable merely because it can administer or absorb consequences generated elsewhere.</p> <p>Where the relevant condition exists only through relations among several authorities—such as a watershed, grid, labour market, settlement network, transport corridor, or shared infrastructure—authority may need to be jointly constituted rather than described as a local power transferred upward.</p> <h2>2.12 Duty attached to retained jurisdiction</h2> <p>Every consequential referral shall disclose whether a response duty exists and identify its source and effect. A response duty may arise through law, treaty, charter, compact, contract, valid internal rule, or voluntary commitment; this Specification does not create that duty by itself.</p> <p>Conforming constituting instruments should establish a reviewable duty for an institution retaining jurisdiction to:</p> <ul><li>acknowledge a properly routed request;</li> <li>accept, refuse, defer, or refer it within a decision-appropriate period;</li> <li>provide reasons and identify conflicts;</li> <li>preserve evidence and protected minimums during delay;</li> <li>maintain an appeal or escalation route.</li></ul> <p>Delay or refusal does not automatically transfer authority. It does not erase responsibility for the consequences of avoidable non-action.</p> <h2>2.13 Omission, incapacity, and constitutional gaps</h2> <p>The constitutional stack shall distinguish:</p> <ul><li>lawful refusal;</li> <li>unresolved uncertainty;</li> <li>avoidable delay;</li> <li>practical incapacity;</li> <li>deliberate obstruction;</li> <li>lack of evidence;</li> <li>absence of lawful jurisdiction.</li></ul> <p>Where no valid authority possesses sufficient jurisdiction over a consequential condition, the system shall publish a <strong>Constitutional Gap Notice</strong> and route the matter toward lawful compact, legislation, Treaty amendment, adjudication, or another constituting process. The notice identifies the gap; it does not fill it by implication.</p> <h2>2.14 Proportional procedural burden</h2> <p>Record depth and review requirements shall be proportional to:</p> <ul><li>reversibility;</li> <li>rights and service effects;</li> <li>coercion;</li> <li>public-law effect;</li> <li>exposure of non-participants;</li> <li>ecological irreversibility;</li> <li>data sensitivity;</li> <li>duration and scale.</li></ul> <p>Low-risk voluntary learning shall not require closure-grade process. High-consequence action shall not evade safeguards because its method is technically simple or locally popular. Existing valid records should be reused where they satisfy the purpose.</p> <h2>2.15 Conformance is not success</h2> <p>Conformance demonstrates that constitutional and procedural requirements were represented. It does not establish:</p> <ul><li>political legitimacy;</li> <li>causal effectiveness;</li> <li>justice;</li> <li>institutional health;</li> <li>community trust;</li> <li>ecological improvement;</li> <li>success of the framework itself.</li></ul> <p>Consequential mechanisms require independent outcome evidence. Frameworks shall state what findings would justify narrowing, suspending, replacing, or declining to scale their own architecture.</p> <p>Frameworks shall also state the conditions under which their mechanisms are expected to function and the conditions under which they are not. A framework that names no operating assumptions asserts universal applicability, which no architecture can support.</p> <hr> <h1>3. Constitutional actors and legitimacy sources</h1> <h2>3.1 Persons</h2> <p>Persons possess dignity, rights, and claims that do not depend on institutional membership, economic contribution, citizenship, possession of a state-issued identity document, data visibility, or participation in GGF systems.</p> <p>No institution may infer diminished standing from low participation, refusal, missing data, absent conventional documentation, or non-use of a platform.</p> <p>A framework may identify a person sufficiently for a bounded lawful purpose without constituting general legal identity. Recognition for one purpose does not create recognition for all purposes.</p> <h2>3.2 Indigenous peoples and nations</h2> <p>Indigenous peoples and nations possess inherent authority arising outside the GGF.</p> <p>Their own processes determine:</p> <ul><li>political representation;</li> <li>territorial authority;</li> <li>consent;</li> <li>knowledge stewardship;</li> <li>internal accountability;</li> <li>relations among nations, clans, communities, and authorities;</li> <li>whether and how to engage the GGF.</li></ul> <p>The GGF may recognize an Indigenous-convened Earth Council, confederation, council of nations, territorial authority, or other institution. It may not presume that one such body represents all Indigenous peoples or non-participating nations.</p> <h2>3.3 Democratic territorial authorities</h2> <p>Territorial councils, municipalities, BAZ assemblies, regional public bodies, states, and treaty organizations exercise place-based public authority only to the extent lawfully constituted.</p> <p>Residence creates standing in territorial decisions but does not grant unilateral control over:</p> <ul><li>cross-boundary commons;</li> <li>Indigenous territories or rights;</li> <li>professional self-governance;</li> <li>externalized harms;</li> <li>treaty obligations.</li></ul> <h2>3.4 Commons institutions</h2> <p>Commons Trusts, watershed councils, ocean trusts, forest guardianships, atmospheric bodies, and equivalent institutions exercise fiduciary authority over defined ecological or shared systems.</p> <p>Their legitimacy may derive from:</p> <ul><li>affected jurisdictions;</li> <li>rights-of-nature law;</li> <li>treaty authority;</li> <li>Indigenous law and stewardship;</li> <li>public trust law;</li> <li>compact.</li></ul> <p>They shall state the commons governed, affected parties, Guardian roles, evidence methods, rights, limits, and appeal route.</p> <h2>3.5 Practice, labour, and professional institutions</h2> <p>Guilds, syndicates, Fractal Labor Parliament bodies, professional colleges, worker associations, and Community Provider governance bodies may set standards within their legitimate domain.</p> <p>They shall not acquire unilateral authority over:</p> <ul><li>currency issuance;</li> <li>public taxation;</li> <li>territorial land use;</li> <li>ecological limits outside their mandate;</li> <li>individual AUBI rights;</li> <li>closed-shop exclusion contrary to rights.</li></ul> <h2>3.6 Treaty parties and Treaty institutions</h2> <p>Treaty institutions exercise only powers validly conferred by the Treaty after its entry into force for the relevant parties and jurisdiction.</p> <p>Treaty authority shall identify:</p> <ul><li>the parties bound;</li> <li>the territorial and subject scope;</li> <li>the decision rule;</li> <li>non-member effects;</li> <li>rights and reservations;</li> <li>review and amendment;</li> <li>withdrawal and surviving duties.</li></ul> <h2>3.7 Meta-Governance institutions</h2> <p>Meta-Governance bodies are interface institutions. Their default authority is to:</p> <ul><li>map interdependence;</li> <li>convene legitimate authorities;</li> <li>facilitate coordination;</li> <li>maintain interface protocols;</li> <li>support plural observation;</li> <li>publish advisory audits;</li> <li>incubate authorized experiments;</li> <li>preserve institutional learning.</li></ul> <p>They possess no inherent authority to tax, legislate, sanction, dissolve, transfer functions, create a successor government, or override a domain institution.</p> <h2>3.8 Tribunals and adjudicators</h2> <p>Tribunals decide only matters within valid jurisdiction.</p> <p>They may adjudicate:</p> <ul><li>treaty compliance;</li> <li>rights;</li> <li>procedure;</li> <li>jurisdiction;</li> <li>evidence admissibility;</li> <li>compact obligations;</li> <li>restitution.</li></ul> <p>They shall not claim authority to determine the validity of sacred knowledge or replace Indigenous law in matters reserved to Indigenous jurisdiction.</p> <h2>3.9 Guardians, auditors, and adversarial bodies</h2> <p>Guardians, ecological observers, the Office of the Adversary, ombuds institutions, auditors, and citizen review bodies strengthen challenge capacity.</p> <p>Unless separately authorized, they may:</p> <ul><li>inspect;</li> <li>warn;</li> <li>publish dissent;</li> <li>request reconsideration;</li> <li>initiate review;</li> <li>seek an injunction.</li></ul> <p>They do not acquire executive authority merely by identifying a risk.</p> <hr> <h1>4. Functional polycentrism</h1> <h2>4.1 Constitutional topology</h2> <p>The GGF shall use overlapping functional jurisdictions rather than one nested chain of command.</p> <p>The four principal constitutional functions are:</p> <ol><li><strong>Territorial authority — the Where:</strong> place-based infrastructure, public services, land use, and local civic administration.</li> <li><strong>Commons authority — the What:</strong> ecological systems, shared resources, rights of nature, and cross-boundary regenerative limits.</li> <li><strong>Practice and labour authority — the How:</strong> professional standards, worker rights, occupational governance, and practice-specific integrity.</li> <li><strong>Indigenous sovereign authority — the Root:</strong> inherent territorial, cultural, legal, spiritual, and knowledge authority exercised through Indigenous-determined institutions.</li></ol> <p>The fourth is not a GGF chamber and is not reducible to a universal “Wisdom Council.” It is an interface with one or more sovereign authorities whose form is determined outside the GGF.</p> <h2>4.2 Horizontal and vertical checks</h2> <p>Polycentrism requires both:</p> <ul><li><strong>vertical subsidiarity:</strong> authority remains at the lowest capable scale; and</li> <li><strong>horizontal separation:</strong> no territorial, commons, economic, epistemic, or coordinating body controls the others’ domains.</li></ul> <p>Overlap raises the cost of capture and creates alternative routes for challenge. It does not make tyranny, corruption, error, collusion, or capture impossible.</p> <h2>4.3 No unilateral cross-domain action</h2> <p>A body may act independently within its uncontested jurisdiction.</p> <p>Where an action materially affects another domain, the initiating body shall:</p> <ul><li>notify affected authorities;</li> <li>identify the cross-domain effect;</li> <li>seek required consent or review;</li> <li>publish an interface record;</li> <li>provide challenge and adjudication routes.</li></ul> <p>Consultation is insufficient where the other authority possesses a lawful veto, consent right, or exclusive jurisdiction.</p> <h2>4.4 Cross-boundary commons</h2> <p>Commons boundaries shall follow the relevant ecological or functional system rather than defaulting to political borders.</p> <p>A cross-boundary commons institution shall include:</p> <ul><li>upstream and downstream communities;</li> <li>directly affected jurisdictions;</li> <li>Indigenous peoples with territorial or stewardship rights;</li> <li>rights-of-nature or Guardian representation where lawfully constituted;</li> <li>relevant scientific and local knowledge;</li> <li>missing or weakly represented populations.</li></ul> <p>No affected party shall be made fully legible as the price of standing.</p> <h2>4.5 Guardian authority</h2> <p>Guardian roles shall be defined through fiduciary duty to the represented ecosystem or future interest.</p> <p>A Guardian may possess a binding vote or injunction power where lawfully constituted. The scope shall be limited to the represented interest.</p> <p>Disagreement among Guardians, Indigenous authorities, scientists, communities, and territorial bodies shall not be resolved by ranking knowledge systems in a universal hierarchy. The process shall surface:</p> <ul><li>the claims;</li> <li>provenance;</li> <li>uncertainty;</li> <li>incompatible interpretations;</li> <li>urgency;</li> <li>reversibility;</li> <li>protected knowledge boundaries.</li></ul> <p>Where irreversible ecological harm is plausible and evidence remains unresolved, a precautionary temporary hold may apply while independent review continues.</p> <h2>4.6 Fractal adaptation</h2> <p>Small communities may combine institutional roles where separate bodies would be impractical.</p> <p>Role combination shall be disclosed through an Observation Independence Map. A person or body acting as observer, proposer, implementer, and reviewer shall not be treated as independent merely because the functions have different names.</p> <p>The logical distinction among jurisdictions must survive institutional compression.</p> <h2>4.7 Jointly constituted authority</h2> <p>Some conditions are not wholly internal to any participating jurisdiction. Their governance object exists in the relationship among jurisdictions.</p> <p>For such conditions, the parties may constitute a bounded shared authority through compact, treaty, law, Indigenous agreement, commons institution, or another valid process. The constituting instrument shall define:</p> <ul><li>the shared condition;</li> <li>parties and affected non-parties;</li> <li>causal variables and responsibilities;</li> <li>powers and prohibitions;</li> <li>contribution and resource duties;</li> <li>representation and challenge;</li> <li>exit and surviving obligations;</li> <li>review, sunset, and dissolution.</li></ul> <p>Joint constitution is not evidence that the shared body possesses residual authority beyond the relationship for which it was created.</p> <hr> <h1>5. Installation, accession, and non-member status</h1> <h2>5.1 Four authority conditions</h2> <p>The constitutional stack shall distinguish:</p> <h3>A. Voluntary coalition action</h3> <p>Participating actors coordinate only through consent and compact. Non-participants are not bound, except by generally applicable law or duties concerning direct harms.</p> <h3>B. Treaty-party authority</h3> <p>Ratifying parties delegate defined powers to Treaty institutions. The institution may bind only within the conferred jurisdiction.</p> <h3>C. Shared-commons authority</h3> <p>An institution may regulate a shared commons where validly constituted by affected authorities, treaty, law, or recognized rights-of-nature arrangement. The geographic scope follows the commons, not political convenience.</p> <h3>D. Fundamental-rights and international-crime jurisdiction</h3> <p>Some duties may apply irrespective of consent under valid international or constitutional law. The source, threshold, jurisdiction, procedure, and remedies must be explicit.</p> <p>No framework may move silently from A to D.</p> <h2>5.2 Entry into force</h2> <p>A future Treaty shall define:</p> <ul><li>required ratifications;</li> <li>population, regional, or other thresholds;</li> <li>whether institutions activate incrementally;</li> <li>provisional institutions;</li> <li>authority before full entry into force;</li> <li>treatment of reservations;</li> <li>review of democratic legitimacy.</li></ul> <h2>5.3 Non-members</h2> <p>Non-members may not be treated as members through interface design.</p> <p>The Treaty may affect non-members only through valid:</p> <ul><li>international law;</li> <li>border or trade measures within member jurisdiction;</li> <li>response to direct cross-boundary harm;</li> <li>shared-commons arrangements;</li> <li>emergency action lawfully protecting persons or ecosystems.</li></ul> <p>Every non-member effect shall state its legal basis, proportionality, and challenge route.</p> <h2>5.4 Accession and withdrawal</h2> <p>Accession shall be informed, public, and lawfully authorized.</p> <p>Withdrawal shall define:</p> <ul><li>notice;</li> <li>continuing rights and obligations;</li> <li>protection of residents and minorities;</li> <li>treatment of shared assets and records;</li> <li>unresolved claims;</li> <li>ecological and reparative obligations that survive;</li> <li>transition of essential services.</li></ul> <p>Withdrawal shall not erase responsibility for prior harm.</p> <h2>5.5 Indigenous participation</h2> <p>No Treaty accession by a state automatically constitutes Indigenous consent to measures affecting Indigenous territories, rights, knowledge, or institutions.</p> <p>Affected Indigenous peoples retain their own standing and consent rights according to Indigenous law and applicable legal standards.</p> <hr> <h1>6. Decision classes and jurisdiction</h1> <p>Every consequential proposal shall be assigned a decision class before authorization.</p> <h2>6.1 Class I — Primarily local decision</h2> <p><strong>Examples:</strong> ordinary local services, neighborhood infrastructure, internal provider operations, cultural programmes without material external effects.</p> <p><strong>Default authority:</strong> local territorial, community, provider, or Indigenous authority.</p> <p><strong>Upward role:</strong> support, interoperability, or rights review only.</p> <p><strong>Presumption:</strong> no higher-scale override.</p> <h2>6.2 Class II — Voluntary cross-boundary coordination</h2> <p><strong>Examples:</strong> shared procurement, mutual aid, bilateral BAZ trade, shared technical services, voluntary regional compacts.</p> <p><strong>Default authority:</strong> participating parties through compact.</p> <p><strong>Required safeguards:</strong> scope, contribution, exit, dispute process, data limits, sunset, and treatment of non-parties.</p> <p><strong>Presumption:</strong> no binding effect on non-participants.</p> <h2>6.3 Class III — Material cross-boundary externality</h2> <p><strong>Examples:</strong> upstream pollution, contagious disease, cross-border labour exploitation, harmful data or financial spillovers.</p> <p><strong>Default authority:</strong> affected jurisdictions and rights holders seek a compact or adjudicated remedy.</p> <p><strong>Upward intervention condition:</strong> credible evidence that local action or voluntary coordination cannot adequately prevent the material harm.</p> <p><strong>Remedy:</strong> targeted to the externality; local autonomy otherwise preserved.</p> <h2>6.4 Class IV — Shared planetary commons</h2> <p><strong>Examples:</strong> atmosphere, high seas, migratory species, orbital environment, globally consequential biodiversity systems.</p> <p><strong>Default authority:</strong> validly constituted commons or Treaty institutions with affected-party and Indigenous standing.</p> <p><strong>Required safeguards:</strong> plural observation, planetary and local impact, distribution, historical responsibility, rights, and benefit sharing.</p> <h2>6.5 Class V — Fundamental rights and international crimes</h2> <p><strong>Examples:</strong> genocide, slavery, torture, severe persecution, ecocide where legally defined, systematic denial of fundamental rights.</p> <p><strong>Default authority:</strong> competent courts and enforcement bodies under valid law.</p> <p><strong>Required safeguards:</strong> legality, jurisdiction, evidence, defence, independent adjudication, proportional remedy, and non-weaponization review.</p> <h2>6.6 Class VI — Indigenous territory, knowledge, culture, and sacred relations</h2> <p><strong>Examples:</strong> development on Indigenous territory, access to sacred knowledge, research using Traditional Knowledge, representation of Indigenous authority, cultural heritage, data concerning Indigenous communities.</p> <p><strong>Default authority:</strong> affected Indigenous peoples through their own institutions.</p> <p><strong>GGF role:</strong> fulfill its own obligations, provide resources when invited, recognize bounded attestations, and coordinate with other valid jurisdictions.</p> <p><strong>Prohibition:</strong> state, Treaty, BAZ, Commons Trust, Meta-Governance, or GGF-defined council may substitute for affected-nation authority.</p> <h2>6.7 Emergency overlay</h2> <p>A crisis does not become a separate permanent jurisdiction. It temporarily modifies process within the limits of Section 13.</p> <p>The emergency record shall identify the underlying decision class. A crisis label cannot convert a Class VI matter into unrestricted global jurisdiction.</p> <hr> <h1>7. Causal, Economic, and Constitutional Subsidiarity Test</h1> <p>Before authority moves upward, sideways, or into a new body, the proposer shall complete a Causal, Economic, and Constitutional Subsidiarity Test.</p> <h2>7.1 Required questions</h2> <ol><li>What condition is being addressed?</li> <li>Who is affected, including absent and future parties?</li> <li>Which authority currently holds jurisdiction?</li> <li>What cross-boundary harm, rights issue, or commons duty exists?</li> <li>What evidence supports the condition?</li> <li>What materially different explanations remain plausible?</li> <li>Has the current authority received sufficient resources, time, and technical support?</li> <li>Could voluntary compact, shared service, mutual aid, or narrower remedy suffice?</li> <li>What is the least additional authority required?</li> <li>What local discretion remains protected?</li> <li>What rights, knowledge, and material minimums remain protected?</li> <li>What is the duration?</li> <li>Who may challenge the transfer?</li> <li>What are the principal causal variables, and who controls each one legally and practically?</li> <li>Which burdens, resources, and risks move with the proposed responsibility?</li> <li>Which upstream or external duties remain with actors retaining causal control?</li> <li>Does any necessary function lack a competent authority?</li> <li>What outcome would falsify the transfer’s necessity?</li> <li>When and how does authority return?</li></ol> <h2>7.2 Capacity-first rule</h2> <p>Administrative weakness shall not automatically justify centralization.</p> <p>Before transferring authority, consider:</p> <ul><li>capacity grants;</li> <li>shared services;</li> <li>technical assistance;</li> <li>temporary delegation;</li> <li>federated administration;</li> <li>independent observers;</li> <li>slower implementation;</li> <li>mutual aid.</li></ul> <p>Resource inequality must not become an automatic hierarchy of sovereignty.</p> <h2>7.3 Failure-to-act standard</h2> <p>A lower-scale authority “demonstrably fails to act” only where:</p> <ul><li>it possesses jurisdiction and practical capability or has refused reasonable support;</li> <li>a defined material harm or rights duty exists;</li> <li>time and process are proportionate to urgency;</li> <li>evidence and uncertainty are published;</li> <li>the authority can respond and contest;</li> <li>where the remedy constrains or overrides the allegedly failing authority, a competent body independent of that failure authorizes the remedy.</li></ul> <p>Failure is not established solely by missing a performance target, rejecting a Meta-Governance recommendation, preserving a different ontology, or declining to adopt a preferred policy.</p> <h2>7.4 Causal Responsibility Profile</h2> <p>A consequential transfer, devolution, compact, reform mandate, or system-effect pilot shall identify:</p> <ol><li>the condition and principal causal variables;</li> <li>the legal and practical controller of each variable;</li> <li>affected persons, communities, nations, ecosystems, and non-members;</li> <li>responsibility assigned to each actor;</li> <li>resources and authority accompanying that responsibility;</li> <li>upstream and external dependencies;</li> <li>burdens that would otherwise be absorbed locally;</li> <li>shared or concurrent jurisdiction;</li> <li>variables no current institution controls;</li> <li>continuing duties of actors retaining control;</li> <li>escalation and remedy;</li> <li>conclusions the proposed action cannot establish.</li></ol> <p>A body shall not be deemed capable solely because it can administer consequences.</p> <h2>7.5 Impossible mandate or pilot</h2> <p>An institution or community may issue an <strong>Impossible Mandate or Pilot Notice</strong> where:</p> <ul><li>the required outcome exceeds its lawful authority or practical action set;</li> <li>resources are materially insufficient;</li> <li>decisive causal variables remain controlled elsewhere;</li> <li>contradictory duties cannot be reconciled;</li> <li>the proposed pilot is too narrow to test the mechanism it claims;</li> <li>compliance would shift unmanaged harm onto workers, households, communities, ecosystems, or future parties.</li></ul> <p>The notice triggers review and responsibility mapping. It does not automatically remove the institution’s existing authority or duties.</p> <h2>7.6 Response, refusal, and omission</h2> <p>For consequential referrals, findings, and requests, the governing interface shall preserve at least:</p> <ul><li>the candidate duty-holder and basis for competence;</li> <li>the source and effect of any response duty;</li> <li>the legal, charter, compact, or response-time basis for acknowledgment and decision clocks;</li> <li>the response, reasons, evidence, conflicts, and missing prerequisites;</li> <li>harm expected during delay;</li> <li>provisional safe-state authority where one exists;</li> <li>sender and recipient duties;</li> <li>escalation, review, and unresolved-duty custody;</li> <li>whether the issue is capacity-limited or lacks lawful jurisdiction.</li></ul> <p>Exact controlled response states, field names, clock semantics, and lifecycle transitions are governed by the current compatible GMEAIA release. Silence does not create acceptance, correctness, or authority transfer.</p> <hr> <h1>8. Refusal, consent, veto, and injunction</h1> <h2>8.1 Distinct constitutional acts</h2> <p>The following shall remain distinct:</p> <ul><li><strong>Non-participation:</strong> declining to join an institution or process.</li> <li><strong>Withdrawal:</strong> ending participation under defined terms.</li> <li><strong>Adaptation:</strong> implementing a shared rule differently within allowed discretion.</li> <li><strong>Refusal:</strong> declining an act, disclosure, or role.</li> <li><strong>Consent:</strong> affirmative authorization where required.</li> <li><strong>Veto:</strong> lawful power to prevent a decision within a defined jurisdiction.</li> <li><strong>Injunction:</strong> temporary or permanent order stopping an act to protect rights or prevent harm.</li> <li><strong>Emergency hold:</strong> short reversible containment pending review.</li> <li><strong>Reconsideration brake:</strong> requirement that another body reconsider without predetermining the result.</li></ul> <h2>8.2 Veto scope</h2> <p>A veto shall identify:</p> <ul><li>holder;</li> <li>constituting authority;</li> <li>jurisdiction;</li> <li>decision stopped;</li> <li>legal or constitutional basis;</li> <li>evidence or bounded attestation;</li> <li>duration;</li> <li>review or renewal;</li> <li>remedy required for release.</li></ul> <p>No universal quota shall dictate how often a legitimate veto may be exercised. A numerical target for “healthy veto frequency” is not a constitutional standard.</p> <h2>8.3 Indigenous consent and veto</h2> <p>Where Indigenous consent is required, non-consent is not merely advice and cannot be overridden through a general popularity vote, sortition jury, global representation quota, or claim of system optimization.</p> <p>The GGF may adjudicate whether its own institutions followed required procedure. It may not declare protected Indigenous reasons invalid because they were not fully disclosed or translated into a preferred evidentiary form.</p> <h2>8.4 Commons veto</h2> <p>A Commons Trust or Guardian veto shall be limited to material effects on the commons or represented ecological interest.</p> <p>It may stop or condition the harmful activity. It shall not automatically acquire general territorial, economic, or criminal authority.</p> <h2>8.5 Consequence proportionality</h2> <p>A veto or charter revocation shall target the responsible act, permission, institution, or authority as closely as possible.</p> <p>It shall not automatically:</p> <ul><li>invalidate personal balances;</li> <li>suspend AUBI;</li> <li>terminate essential care;</li> <li>impose collective punishment;</li> <li>erase records;</li> <li>expose protected knowledge;</li> <li>collapse unrelated economic activity.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>9. Decision observability and epistemic governance</h1> <h2>9.1 Consequential decisions</h2> <p>A decision is consequential where it may materially affect:</p> <ul><li>rights;</li> <li>sovereignty;</li> <li>territory;</li> <li>essential services;</li> <li>ecological integrity;</li> <li>economic access;</li> <li>institutional existence;</li> <li>protected knowledge;</li> <li>crisis powers;</li> <li>significant public resources.</li></ul> <p>Consequential decisions require the records in this section.</p> <h2>9.2 Observation Contract</h2> <p>An Observation Contract shall state:</p> <ul><li>decision class;</li> <li>latent condition of concern;</li> <li>observations used;</li> <li>observations unavailable;</li> <li>sources and dependencies;</li> <li>the information class and constituting purpose of each material source;</li> <li>whether each material use is primary, compatible secondary, or consequential secondary use;</li> <li>where a recognition record is proposed for consequential secondary use, the independent authority, necessity, and less-intrusive-source analysis required by §10.4;</li> <li>any material transformation by which a narrative, contribution record, credential, attestation, activity record, or relational record becomes an input to a financial, eligibility, risk, compliance, or enforcement inference;</li> <li>population coverage;</li> <li>missing-population risk;</li> <li>whether the observation depends on a voluntary act of disclosure, and by whom;</li> <li>what the discloser risks by disclosing, where it does;</li> <li>protected knowledge boundaries;</li> <li>uncertainty;</li> <li>time scale and lag;</li> <li>model or interpretive method;</li> <li>alternative interpretations;</li> <li>sensor-change rules;</li> <li>independent outcomes;</li> <li>correction and challenge routes.</li></ul> <p>A data source does not become constitutionally suitable for a decision merely because it is available, technically interoperable, accurate for its original purpose, or convenient to reuse.</p> <h2>9.3 State Estimate Record</h2> <p>Before authorization, the responsible interpreter shall publish:</p> <ul><li>leading state estimate;</li> <li>coexisting conditions;</li> <li>plausible alternatives;</li> <li>incompatible explanations;</li> <li>confidence and uncertainty;</li> <li>evidence for and against each;</li> <li>source-dependence concerns;</li> <li>consequences of acting under the wrong explanation;</li> <li>whether the cause remains unresolved.</li></ul> <p>“Cause unresolved” is a valid constitutional state.</p> <h2>9.4 Observation Independence Map</h2> <p>The decision record shall disclose whether observations share:</p> <ul><li>personnel;</li> <li>funders;</li> <li>platforms;</li> <li>databases;</li> <li>incentives;</li> <li>algorithms;</li> <li>governance authorities;</li> <li>cultural or methodological assumptions.</li></ul> <p>Several reports generated by one institutional pipeline do not constitute independent evidence.</p> <h2>9.4.1 Common-mode dependency review</h2> <p>For high-consequence decisions, disclosure shall be supplemented by a review of whether one shared dependency could distort or disable several channels simultaneously.</p> <p>The review should address:</p> <ul><li>common data and identity infrastructure;</li> <li>AI models and model providers;</li> <li>cloud, platform, settlement, or communications dependencies;</li> <li>shared funders and appointment authorities;</li> <li>common professional or methodological assumptions;</li> <li>fallback channels and local continuity;</li> <li>the decision that remains possible if the dominant observation stack fails.</li></ul> <p>Where genuine independence is not feasible, the competent authority shall record its acceptance of the residual correlation risk. Organizational separation alone does not establish independence.</p> <h2>9.5 Epistemic plurality</h2> <p>Major planetary, intergenerational, rights, or institutional-replacement decisions should use several genuinely independent interpretive systems where feasible.</p> <p>Plurality is not satisfied by running several models on the same data and ontology.</p> <p>The process shall preserve disagreement rather than averaging it into one recommendation. Decision-makers may act under uncertainty but must state why the selected action remains justified across the unresolved interpretations.</p> <h2>9.6 No cultural scoring</h2> <p>Central bodies shall not assign a universal “cultural adaptation score,” “Indigenous alignment score,” “community sincerity score,” or equivalent metric.</p> <p>Affected communities may provide narratives, conditions, consent decisions, or bounded attestations. These shall not be collapsed into a scalar of cultural compatibility.</p> <h2>9.7 Metrics and sentinels</h2> <p>Composite health indexes, trust scores, veto-balance targets, participation thresholds, learning-velocity ratios, and capture indicators may be exploratory dashboards.</p> <p>They shall not directly trigger:</p> <ul><li>chamber dissolution;</li> <li>exchange-rate penalties;</li> <li>trade isolation;</li> <li>authority transfer;</li> <li>Treaty enforcement;</li> <li>finding of capture;</li> <li>Indigenous intervention.</li></ul> <p>Indicators trigger inquiry. Constitutional consequences require diagnosis and authorization.</p> <h2>9.8 Disclosure-dependent observation</h2> <p>Where an observation reaches the decision-maker only because a person chose to report, disclose, present, enrol, complain, or seek help, the channel measures two things at once: the underlying condition, and the willingness to enter the channel. It cannot separate them.</p> <p>An intervention that changes that willingness therefore changes the measurement independently of the condition, and may move the indicator in the direction opposite to the truth. Restoring trust in a complaint route raises recorded complaints. Making a service feel unsafe, costly, or dangerous lowers recorded need. Neither movement is evidence about the condition.</p> <p>This is distinct from §11.4. Self-validation concerns a control’s mechanical effect on its own trigger. This section concerns whether the population remains willing to be observed at all.</p> <p>For any consequential decision relying on a disclosure-dependent channel, the Observation Contract shall additionally record:</p> <ul><li>the act of disclosure required, and who must perform it;</li> <li>what the discloser risks by disclosing — loss of service, status, employment, housing, care of a child, immigration consequence, retaliation, cost, stigma, or exposure of protected knowledge;</li> <li>whether the intervention under evaluation is expected to raise or lower disclosure, and on what reasoning;</li> <li>an independent channel that does not depend on the same act of disclosure, or a statement that none exists.</li></ul> <p>Where no independent channel exists, movement in the disclosure-dependent indicator is a prompt for inquiry and shall not be recorded as an outcome.</p> <p>No framework shall:</p> <ul><li>set a target for a reduction in recorded reports, complaints, incidents, claims, or requests for help;</li> <li>condition funding, authorization, stage progression, charter renewal, or continuation on such a reduction;</li> <li>treat a fall in recorded harm as evidence of reduced harm where the responsible institution also controls access to the reporting route.</li></ul> <p>Rising recorded disclosure in a channel whose accessibility, safety, language coverage, or trust has recently improved is an expected consequence of that improvement. It is not by itself an adverse finding about the institution, the population, or the condition.</p> <p>An institution that becomes easier to reach will look worse before it looks better. A system that reads the first movement as failure will select for institutions that are hard to reach.</p> <hr> <h1>10. Knowledge, data, and institutional memory</h1> <h2>10.1 Information classes</h2> <h3>Class A — Public constitutional and institutional information</h3> <p>Examples:</p> <ul><li>legal authority;</li> <li>decisions;</li> <li>budgets;</li> <li>institutional rules;</li> <li>public reasons;</li> <li>conflicts of interest;</li> <li>aggregate performance.</li></ul> <p>Public institutions have a strong transparency duty.</p> <h3>Class B — Purpose-limited operational information</h3> <p>Examples:</p> <ul><li>system capacity;</li> <li>aggregate flows;</li> <li>case status;</li> <li>interface performance;</li> <li>ecological observations approved for sharing.</li></ul> <p>Access and retention shall follow purpose limitation.</p> <h3>Class C — Personal and claimant-controlled information</h3> <p>Examples:</p> <ul><li>identity;</li> <li>health;</li> <li>household circumstances;</li> <li>contribution evidence;</li> <li>location;</li> <li>migration status.</li></ul> <p>Individuals retain notice, access, correction, explanation, and appeal rights.</p> <p>A recognition record under §10.4 is Class C where it is personal or claimant-controlled.</p> <h3>Class D — Community-controlled and Indigenous-restricted knowledge</h3> <p>Examples:</p> <ul><li>Traditional Knowledge;</li> <li>community research data;</li> <li>territorial observations;</li> <li>culturally restricted records;</li> <li>language resources;</li> <li>collective histories.</li></ul> <p>The relevant community or Indigenous authority governs access, use, storage, benefit sharing, and withdrawal.</p> <p>A recognition record under §10.4 is Class D where it is constituted and held as a community-controlled or Indigenous-restricted record.</p> <h3>Class E — Sacred, non-transferable, or intentionally unrecorded knowledge</h3> <p>This information may remain outside GGF systems entirely.</p> <p>A bounded conclusion, condition, veto, or attestation may substitute for disclosure.</p> <p>Where an activity or relationship is intentionally unrecorded or protected from transfer, the absence of a recognition record does not create an adverse inference.</p> <h2>10.2 Federated memory</h2> <p>The constitutional stack shall not require one blockchain-backed UN database or universal global ledger.</p> <p>Institutional memory should be federated through:</p> <ul><li>local custody;</li> <li>interoperable provenance;</li> <li>purpose-specific registries;</li> <li>retention limits;</li> <li>deletion or withdrawal where lawful;</li> <li>cryptographic verification where appropriate but not mandatory;</li> <li>offline and non-digital records;</li> <li>protected archives.</li></ul> <h2>10.3 Open-source boundary</h2> <p>Publicly consequential algorithms, model logic, transformation rules, and institutional software should be open and auditable.</p> <p>Open-source obligations do not require publication of:</p> <ul><li>personal data;</li> <li>security-sensitive details;</li> <li>protected Indigenous knowledge;</li> <li>sacred knowledge;</li> <li>confidential witness material.</li></ul> <p>An auditable system may rely on bounded attestations and independent trusted review rather than universal disclosure.</p> <h2>10.4 Recognition-record purpose firewall</h2> <p>A <strong>recognition record</strong> is a record whose constituting purpose is primarily to acknowledge, narrate, validate, attest, allocate recognition for, or support participation in care, ecological stewardship, gratitude, relational activity, civic or community participation, cultural contribution, mutual aid, voluntary contribution, or analogous social, ecological, cultural, or community activity.</p> <p>A recognition record is governed by the information class applicable to its holder and content. It is normally Class C when personal or claimant-controlled and Class D when community-controlled or Indigenous-restricted. Protected absence, non-recording, or non-transfer may also be valid under Class E.</p> <p>The existence, availability, interoperability, or predictive usefulness of a recognition record does not create authority to reuse it outside its constituting purpose.</p> <p>Unless the requirements in §10.4.1 are satisfied, a recognition record shall not be converted into or used as an input to:</p> <ul><li>wealth or taxable-capacity estimation;</li> <li>creditworthiness, insurance-risk, or generalized financial-risk scoring;</li> <li>employment, promotion, labour-access, migration, residency, or citizenship decisions;</li> <li>fraud, abuse, security, or law-enforcement risk scoring;</li> <li>political-reliability, ideological-conformity, participant-reputation, or generalized social-credit scoring;</li> <li>unrelated benefit, service, licence, procurement, sanction, or eligibility decisions.</li></ul> <p>This rule applies to direct use and to derived use through data fusion, embeddings, inferred features, identity matching, aggregate proxies, or another transformation. It supplements §17.3; it does not create a parallel reputation system.</p> <h3>10.4.1 Consequential secondary use</h3> <p>A consequential secondary use of a recognition record requires all of the following:</p> <ol><li><p><strong>Independent authority and defined purpose.</strong> The receiving institution shall identify an independently valid constituting basis for the secondary use, the specific decision it supports, and the relationship of that decision to the institution’s mandate. The original record does not supply that authority.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Necessity and less-intrusive-source test.</strong> The institution shall show that the information is materially necessary and that the required fact cannot reasonably be established through a source constituted for that purpose, a bounded attestation, or another materially less intrusive observation channel. Predictive usefulness, lower cost, or administrative convenience is insufficient.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Minimum necessary use and purpose-change provenance.</strong> Only the information necessary for the authorized purpose may be transferred or derived. The record shall preserve the original purpose, new purpose, receiving authority, material transformation, retention rule, and applicable restrictions.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Applicable rights and protected boundaries.</strong> Privacy, consent, data sovereignty, Indigenous authority, protected non-transfer, worker and claimant rights, and other domain safeguards continue to apply. Consent alone is insufficient where material dependency or public authority makes refusal practically unavailable.</p></li> <li><p><strong>Contestability and protected absence.</strong> Materially affected persons or authorities shall receive the applicable notice, access, correction, explanation, challenge, and remedy. Refusal to participate, protected non-transfer, intentional non-recording, lawful deletion, or absence of recognition records shall not itself become an adverse signal.</p></li></ol> <p>Nothing in this subsection allows a recognition record or an inference derived from it to bypass the decision-chain, automatic-control, adjudication, or challenge requirements elsewhere in this Specification.</p> <h3>10.4.2 Financial and operational record at creation</h3> <p>Where a recognized activity lawfully generates a financial asset, payment, entitlement, liability, ownership interest, settlement event, or other consequential operational fact, the responsible domain system shall create a purpose-appropriate financial or operational record at the point that fact is constituted.</p> <p>Where responsibility for creating that record is disputed or spans more than one domain framework, the responsible functions shall be allocated through the §18.1 conflict rule before consequential operation. A missing allocation is an interface gap; it does not authorize reconstruction from the recognition record by default.</p> <p>That record shall contain only the information necessary for the financial, accounting, entitlement, settlement, audit, or other authorized purpose and shall preserve provenance sufficient to distinguish it from the underlying recognition record.</p> <p>A later financial, tax, accounting, regulatory, or adjudicative process should use the purpose-appropriate record rather than reconstructing the event from care narratives, Love Ledger histories, contribution evidence, or other recognition records.</p> <p>Failure to create the required purpose-appropriate record is an interface defect requiring correction. It does not by itself authorize unrestricted repurposing of the recognition record. Where valid law specifically requires access to the underlying recognition record, §10.4.1 still applies together with the applicable domain safeguards.</p> <h3>10.4.3 Asset and recognition-record boundary</h3> <p>This Specification does not determine the legal, accounting, fiscal, or regulatory treatment of Hearts, Leaves, currencies, payments, entitlements, ownership interests, or other assets and transactions governed by domain frameworks and valid law.</p> <p>The Recognition-Record Purpose Firewall protects the purpose boundary of the recognition record. It does not make an independently constituted asset, payment, entitlement, liability, ownership interest, or transaction legally invisible merely because it arose from recognized contribution.</p> <p>Conversely, lawful authority over a resulting asset or transaction does not automatically create authority over the care, ecological, civic, cultural, relational, or other recognition record that preceded it.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Origin in recognized contribution does not immunize an independently constituted asset or transaction from valid law. Valid law governing that asset or transaction does not erase the purpose boundary of the recognition record that preceded it.</strong></p></blockquote> <h3>10.4.4 Love Ledger application</h3> <p>Love Ledger narratives, Gratitude Tokens, recognition histories, and related relational records are recognition records for purposes of this section. The prohibitions and conditions in §10.4–§10.4.3 apply in addition to the Love Ledger-specific boundary below.</p> <p>Love Ledger records shall not serve as a default source for:</p> <ul><li>institutional legitimacy;</li> <li>cultural scoring;</li> <li>labour matching;</li> <li>public-sentiment measurement;</li> <li>Treaty enforcement;</li> <li>LMCI-M;</li> <li>economic sanctions.</li></ul> <p>Nothing in this subsection requires a Love Ledger record to exist, become digital, become transferable, or become visible to a financial or public authority.</p> <h2>10.5 Data-change governance</h2> <p>A consequential change in sensors, data sources, classifications, models, or retention shall be versioned and reviewed as a policy change.</p> <p>The record shall explain how the change affects:</p> <ul><li>who becomes visible;</li> <li>who disappears;</li> <li>historical comparability;</li> <li>incentives;</li> <li>privacy;</li> <li>Indigenous and community control;</li> <li>decision thresholds;</li> <li>constituting purpose and any new secondary use;</li> <li>whether a recognition record is being replaced by, or converted into, a purpose-appropriate operational record.</li></ul> <h2>10.6 Person continuity and purpose-limited attestation</h2> <h3>10.6.1 Constitutional doctrine</h3> <blockquote><p><strong>Recognition is not registration. Linkage is not identity. Attestation is not jurisdiction.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>A GGF framework may identify a person sufficiently for its own valid purpose and may, under separately valid authority, recognize that another purpose-limited record concerns the same person. Neither act creates a universal GGF identity, civil registry, citizenship status, legal-personhood credential, or general authority over the person.</p> <p>Aurora governs data-purpose, minimization, anti-linkage, access, correction, retention, and transfer constraints. This Specification supplies only the constitutional grammar required when person continuity must cross framework or institutional boundaries.</p> <h3>10.6.2 Purpose-limited identifiers</h3> <p>Where general civil identity is unnecessary or unavailable, a framework may use a local, provisional, pseudonymous, self-attested, community-attested, witness-supported, attribute-only, or other purpose-limited identifier if valid law or the framework’s lawful constituting instrument permits it.</p> <p>The identifier or attestation shall state or make discoverable:</p> <ul><li>issuing or attesting source;</li> <li>constituting authority or lawful basis;</li> <li>purpose and relying process;</li> <li>scope;</li> <li>evidence class and material uncertainty;</li> <li>permitted relying parties;</li> <li>prohibited uses;</li> <li>validity, expiry, or review condition;</li> <li>correction and challenge route;</li> <li>whether cross-domain continuity recognition is prohibited, separately authorizable, or already authorized for a stated purpose.</li></ul> <p>A purpose-limited identifier shall not silently become a travel document, national identity, immigration credential, voting credential, benefits key, employment identity, healthcare identity, justice identity, financial identity, reputation score, universal authentication token, or permanent cross-domain correlation key.</p> <p>Love Ledger participation, recognition history, contribution evidence, or other purpose-bound recognition records shall not upgrade a purpose-limited identifier into general legal identity.</p> <h3>10.6.3 Same-person continuity assertion</h3> <p>A <strong>Person-Continuity Assertion</strong> states that two or more purpose-limited records are asserted to concern the same person, or that a previously asserted relation is disputed or corrected.</p> <p>The assertion may originate from:</p> <ul><li>the person concerned;</li> <li>a representative lawfully authorized for the relevant purpose;</li> <li>a competent civil, judicial, administrative, Indigenous, community, or other authority;</li> <li>a bounded attester whose role is valid for the specific relying process.</li></ul> <p>The assertion does not merge the source records. It creates only a purpose-limited relationship among them.</p> <p>Before consequential reliance, the receiving body shall identify:</p> <ul><li>the exact records or identifiers being related;</li> <li>the specific decision or continuity purpose;</li> <li>the authority permitting the relation;</li> <li>necessity and a less-intrusive alternative analysis;</li> <li>minimum information required;</li> <li>source provenance and evidence limits;</li> <li>affected-person notice where lawful and safe;</li> <li>correction, mistaken-merge, mistaken-split, duplicate, and collision routes;</li> <li>review or challenge appropriate to consequence;</li> <li>expiry, unlinking, or renewed-authorization conditions.</li></ul> <p>Where the receiving body owes a response duty, acceptance, refusal, deferment, referral, capacity limitation, timeout, misrouting, constitutional-gap handling, and subsequent omission remain governed by GMEAIA rather than by a new identity-specific lifecycle vocabulary.</p> <h3>10.6.4 Non-transitivity and no master identity graph</h3> <p>Same-person recognition is <strong>non-transitive by default</strong>.</p> <p>If Domain A is lawfully linked to Domain B, and Domain B is separately linked to Domain C, Domain A does not thereby acquire authority to access, infer from, or link to Domain C.</p> <p>No GGF framework may construct a universal master identity graph merely by accumulating lawful pairwise continuity assertions.</p> <p>A recipient may rely only on the relationship and fields necessary for its stated purpose. It shall not infer unrelated attributes, participation history, political affiliation, contribution, reputation, migration status, health status, family relation, or other domain facts from the existence of the linkage.</p> <h3>10.6.5 Correction, collision, merge, and split</h3> <p>A continuity architecture must represent at least:</p> <ul><li>mistaken merge — records wrongly treated as one person;</li> <li>mistaken split — one person incorrectly represented as several unrelated persons;</li> <li>duplicate identifier;</li> <li>conflicting same-person assertions;</li> <li>superseded or corrected identifier;</li> <li>unresolved identity relation.</li></ul> <p>Correction shall preserve source provenance. A receiving framework shall not rewrite another authority’s source record merely to make its own linkage internally consistent.</p> <p>Where a source authority corrects its record, downstream correction propagates only to recipients with valid authority to receive the correction. A correction path shall not become a new cross-domain disclosure channel.</p> <p>An unresolved identity relation may limit a specific consequential action where identity is lawfully necessary. It shall not be generalized into denial of unrelated essential assistance or rights.</p> <h3>10.6.6 External civil-status transitions</h3> <p>A GGF framework may receive a civil-status or legal-status transition established by a competent external authority, including a death record, legal-name correction, guardianship change, corrected civil record, or another status event relevant to its lawful function.</p> <p>The receiving framework:</p> <ul><li>records the source authority and effective date;</li> <li>records any material contest, uncertainty, or correction status;</li> <li>applies only consequences within its own lawful mandate;</li> <li>does not register the civil event itself;</li> <li>does not determine inheritance, parentage, citizenship, legal capacity, family status, or another external legal consequence merely from receiving the event;</li> <li>preserves any accrued claims, liabilities, remedies, representation needs, succession questions, and unresolved duties that survive the status transition;</li> <li>routes handoff, unresolved-duty, response, omission, and closure states through GMEAIA where applicable;</li> <li>retains or disposes of data under Aurora and applicable law.</li></ul> <p>A report of inactivity, account dormancy, cessation of contributions, absence from a residence, missing sensor data, non-response, or disappearance from a platform does <strong>not</strong> by itself establish death.</p> <h3>10.6.7 Death and unresolved-duty continuity</h3> <p>An externally established death may end or transform active participation, authority, consent, credentials, service relationships, or account access according to the receiving domain’s law.</p> <p>It shall not automatically:</p> <ul><li>erase accrued benefits or payment claims;</li> <li>erase liabilities or debts;</li> <li>extinguish complaints, remedies, restitution, or compensation claims;</li> <li>determine heirs or successors;</li> <li>delete records needed for lawful succession or accountability;</li> <li>transfer rights to a framework-created successor;</li> <li>close unresolved duties merely because the person is no longer alive.</li></ul> <p>Where a duty, claim, asset, liability, or remedy requires succession, the domain framework records and refers the unresolved question to the competent succession, probate, beneficiary, contractual, Indigenous, customary, judicial, or other authority. The GGF framework gains no succession jurisdiction by making the referral.</p> <h3>10.6.8 Prohibited identity promotion</h3> <p>No framework, funder, platform, registry, credential provider, or coordinating body may treat successful use of a purpose-limited identifier as evidence that the identifier should become mandatory or universal.</p> <p>Scale, interoperability, convenience, fraud reduction, administrative efficiency, or repeated cross-framework acceptance do not create authority to centralize identity.</p> <p>A framework that cannot function without a universal correlation key must treat that dependency as a constitutional design problem rather than silently creating the key.</p> <hr> <h1>11. Automatic controls</h1> <h2>11.1 Permitted automatic functions</h2> <p>Subject to versioning, audit, and human review, automation may:</p> <ul><li>issue an alert;</li> <li>convene a meeting;</li> <li>preserve evidence;</li> <li>isolate a compromised technical node;</li> <li>pause a specific transaction or activity briefly;</li> <li>enforce a previously authorized hard safety limit;</li> <li>maintain a humanitarian minimum;</li> <li>route a case to independent review;</li> <li>publish uncertainty or data failure.</li></ul> <h2>11.2 Prohibited automatic constitutional consequences</h2> <p>Automation shall not independently:</p> <ul><li>determine guilt or capture;</li> <li>transfer jurisdiction;</li> <li>dissolve a chamber or institution;</li> <li>invalidate a BAZ economy;</li> <li>revoke Treaty membership;</li> <li>impose a permanent sanction;</li> <li>redirect protected AUBI;</li> <li>alter constitutional rights;</li> <li>rank Indigenous legitimacy;</li> <li>penalize exchange rates based on reputation;</li> <li>compel knowledge disclosure;</li> <li>authorize armed deployment;</li> <li>convert sovereign debt;</li> <li>create a successor government.</li></ul> <h2>11.3 Containment requirements</h2> <p>An automatic hold shall be:</p> <ul><li>narrow;</li> <li>time-limited;</li> <li>reversible;</li> <li>non-punitive;</li> <li>evidence-preserving;</li> <li>visible to affected parties unless immediate safety requires temporary confidentiality;</li> <li>reviewable by a human authority;</li> <li>accompanied by a protected minimum.</li></ul> <h2>11.4 Self-validating controls</h2> <p>A control’s effect on its trigger indicator does not prove success.</p> <p>Examples:</p> <ul><li>dissolving a chamber reduces measured deadlock;</li> <li>adding sensors raises measured observational dimensionality;</li> <li>trade penalties lower interaction with a sanctioned BAZ;</li> <li>blocking an activity prevents further reported violations;</li> <li>changing a threshold restores formal compliance.</li></ul> <p>Independent human, institutional, and ecological outcomes are required.</p> <hr> <h1>12. Deadlock, mediation, and adjudication</h1> <h2>12.1 Graduated process</h2> <p>A cross-domain deadlock should proceed through:</p> <ol><li>direct negotiation;</li> <li>facilitated mediation;</li> <li>publication of competing State Estimate Records;</li> <li>affected-party deliberation;</li> <li>independent adjudication where rights or jurisdiction are involved;</li> <li>a sortition or citizen body where constitutionally authorized for the specific decision;</li> <li>Treaty or court remedy where applicable.</li></ol> <p>Not every dispute requires every stage. Urgency and harm determine sequencing.</p> <h2>12.2 Timeout defaults</h2> <p>Timeouts may trigger escalation or temporary precautionary protection. They shall not establish which party is substantively correct.</p> <p>Every consequential timeout rule should identify:</p> <ul><li>acknowledgment and decision deadlines;</li> <li>the actor responsible at each handoff;</li> <li>the status assigned to non-response;</li> <li>the escalation route;</li> <li>protected continuity during delay;</li> <li>whether delay itself can create material harm.</li></ul> <p>The status quo is not always neutral. Where continuation creates irreversible harm, the interim default may be a narrow precautionary hold. A missed clock does not transfer jurisdiction automatically, but it shall not disappear as an unowned procedural fact.</p> <h2>12.3 Sortition bodies</h2> <p>A sortition jury may decide a specific deadlock only where:</p> <ul><li>its authority was established before the dispute;</li> <li>the decision is within its jurisdiction;</li> <li>affected rights and Indigenous consent are preserved;</li> <li>members receive balanced independent support;</li> <li>evidence and uncertainty are accessible;</li> <li>conflicts of interest are screened;</li> <li>appeal is available for jurisdiction, rights, or serious procedural error.</li></ul> <p>A sortition decision shall not automatically dissolve every deadlocked chamber or make the jury a temporary general government.</p> <p>Institutional dissolution requires the separate process in Section 16.</p> <h2>12.4 Incentive limits</h2> <p>No Hearts, Leaves, Love Ledger recognition, reputation score, or political-survival threat should reward a particular settlement outcome.</p> <p>Institutions may fund participation and compensate time. They should not pay a “consensus bonus” that makes agreement itself the objective regardless of justice or accuracy.</p> <h2>12.5 Jurisdictional conflicts</h2> <p>Where two authorities claim exclusive jurisdiction, the adjudicator shall first decide:</p> <ul><li>the sources of authority;</li> <li>the decision class;</li> <li>territorial and subject scope;</li> <li>rights and consent;</li> <li>whether shared or concurrent jurisdiction exists.</li></ul> <p>Substantive merits follow only after jurisdiction is clarified.</p> <hr> <h1>13. Emergency Constitutionalism Protocol</h1> <h2>13.1 Emergency definition</h2> <p>An emergency is a time-bounded condition presenting a credible and serious threat to life, fundamental rights, essential systems, or irreversible ecological integrity, where ordinary process cannot respond within the necessary time.</p> <p>Political inconvenience, institutional resistance, slow reform, declining trust, or failure to meet a performance target is not an emergency.</p> <h2>13.2 Provisional action</h2> <p>A narrowly authorized body may take provisional action for no more than 72 hours where delay would cause serious harm.</p> <p>The provisional record shall state:</p> <ul><li>evidence;</li> <li>uncertainty;</li> <li>legal authority;</li> <li>action;</li> <li>affected rights;</li> <li>protected minimums;</li> <li>reason ordinary process was insufficient;</li> <li>review time.</li></ul> <h2>13.3 Full emergency authorization</h2> <p>Continuation beyond 72 hours requires a formal authorization specifying:</p> <ul><li>crisis class;</li> <li>geographic and subject scope;</li> <li>powers activated;</li> <li>powers prohibited;</li> <li>duration;</li> <li>oversight;</li> <li>local and Indigenous interfaces;</li> <li>financial authority;</li> <li>work mobilisation rules;</li> <li>review and termination.</li></ul> <p>An authorization should not exceed 30 days without renewal. Cumulative renewals beyond 90 days require extraordinary independent review and a transition to ordinary law where the condition has become persistent.</p> <h2>13.4 Powers that remain prohibited</h2> <p>Emergency authority shall not automatically permit:</p> <ul><li>suspension of AUBI Layer 1;</li> <li>confiscation of personal balances;</li> <li>compulsory disclosure of sacred knowledge;</li> <li>permanent territorial transfer;</li> <li>permanent constitutional amendment;</li> <li>indefinite detention or censorship;</li> <li>automatic guilt or blacklist findings;</li> <li>permanent institution dissolution;</li> <li>forced labour through Community Providers;</li> <li>irreversible extraction from Indigenous territory.</li></ul> <h2>13.5 Local sensemaking during emergency</h2> <p>Ordinary local interpretation may be compressed only to the minimum required by urgency.</p> <p>Every emergency shall maintain:</p> <ul><li>affected-area liaison;</li> <li>local evidence channels;</li> <li>Indigenous notification and authority where affected;</li> <li>accessible public reasons;</li> <li>post-emergency review;</li> <li>restoration of ordinary jurisdiction.</li></ul> <p>Where immediate rescue of life makes prior consent impossible, action shall be narrow, non-extractive, temporary, and immediately reviewable. It does not create precedent for later permanent use.</p> <h2>13.6 Emergency work</h2> <p>Any mobilisation of individuals or Community Providers shall comply with Work in Liberation’s Emergency Work Mobilisation Record, including voluntariness by default, safety, compensation, refusal, conscientious objection, disability and caregiving accommodation, time limits, and demobilisation.</p> <h2>13.7 Financial emergency</h2> <p>Financial measures shall comply with Financial Systems. Emergency authorities may deploy stabilization and essential-provider support within authorized limits but may not silently bypass protected AUBI obligations or redefine protected balances.</p> <h2>13.8 After-action obligations</h2> <p>Every emergency produces:</p> <ul><li>an independent outcome review;</li> <li>rights and subgroup analysis;</li> <li>record of observation failures;</li> <li>account of emergency powers used;</li> <li>restoration and restitution decisions;</li> <li>sensor and rule changes proposed through ordinary process.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>14. Indigenous covenant and economic continuity</h1> <h2>14.1 Surviving Treaty of the Circle principle</h2> <p>Where a BAZ or economic institution operates on Indigenous territory or through an Indigenous covenant, Indigenous authority shall be structural rather than merely consultative.</p> <p>The covenant may grant affected Indigenous authorities power to:</p> <ul><li>refuse the arrangement;</li> <li>define prohibited activities;</li> <li>require conditions;</li> <li>suspend harmful activity;</li> <li>demand remediation;</li> <li>revoke the BAZ’s covenant or charter standing.</li></ul> <h2>14.2 Unsafe coupling prohibited</h2> <p>The covenant shall not be implemented through one cryptographic token whose revocation automatically invalidates all Hearts, personal balances, essential services, or unrelated economic activity.</p> <p>Indigenous authority should not be made politically responsible for uncontrolled economic collapse.</p> <h2>14.3 Consequence ladder</h2> <p>A covenant violation should proceed, subject to urgency, through:</p> <ol><li>warning or immediate activity-specific hold;</li> <li>evidence preservation and independent observation;</li> <li>Indigenous authority’s finding, condition, or bounded attestation;</li> <li>suspension of the offending permit, project, flow, or institutional power;</li> <li>remediation and restitution plan;</li> <li>charter restriction or revocation;</li> <li>Financial Systems resolution and continuity plan;</li> <li>transfer or reconstitution of lawful functions;</li> <li>restoration if the finding is reversed or obligations are met.</li></ol> <h2>14.4 Protected continuity</h2> <p>Throughout the process:</p> <ul><li>Layer 1 remains protected;</li> <li>innocent personal balances remain valid;</li> <li>essential services continue;</li> <li>workers retain rights and earned compensation;</li> <li>protected knowledge remains under Indigenous control;</li> <li>ecological harm is not allowed to continue merely to preserve financial stability.</li></ul> <h2>14.5 Internal plurality</h2> <p>Where several Indigenous peoples, nations, clans, or authorities hold relevant standing, the GGF shall not select one as the universal representative.</p> <p>The affected peoples determine whether they act through:</p> <ul><li>separate consent decisions;</li> <li>confederation;</li> <li>joint protocol;</li> <li>territorial allocation;</li> <li>consensus;</li> <li>another Indigenous-determined mechanism.</li></ul> <p>Unresolved internal jurisdiction shall be acknowledged rather than solved through GGF sortition or quota design.</p> <hr> <h1>15. Meta-Governance limits and adaptive institutions</h1> <h2>15.1 Connector rule</h2> <p>Meta-Governance may coordinate authority. It does not originate substantive jurisdiction.</p> <p>A Meta-Governance body shall not acquire power over a domain merely because it:</p> <ul><li>detects a problem;</li> <li>holds more data;</li> <li>responds faster;</li> <li>predicts failure;</li> <li>operates a successful pilot;</li> <li>publishes an Accountable Coordination Audit.</li></ul> <h2>15.2 Learning and Adaptation Profile</h2> <p>The scalar Learning Velocity Index and automatic comparison of observational dimensionality to disturbance dimensionality shall be replaced by a plural profile.</p> <p>The profile may include:</p> <ul><li>detection latency;</li> <li>missing-population discovery;</li> <li>source independence;</li> <li>uncertainty calibration;</li> <li>challenge and correction;</li> <li>response latency;</li> <li>implementation capability;</li> <li>independent outcomes;</li> <li>model revision after falsification;</li> <li>ability to reopen decisions;</li> <li>surveillance burden;</li> <li>protected unresolved states;</li> <li>institutional capacity to devolve or dissolve.</li></ul> <p>No composite score shall directly authorize an Adaptive Surge, bypass, funding diversion, functional transfer, or dissolution.</p> <h2>15.3 Variety Gap Assessment</h2> <p>A Variety Gap Assessment is an advisory diagnosis produced through multiple independent perspectives.</p> <p>It shall identify:</p> <ul><li>environment dimensions considered;</li> <li>dimensions excluded;</li> <li>observation channels;</li> <li>authority blind spots;</li> <li>alternative interpretations;</li> <li>whether the apparent gap reflects missing capacity, wrong jurisdiction, missing trust, or deliberate non-observation;</li> <li>recommended responses from least to most intrusive.</li></ul> <p>A finding may convene review. It does not declare “constitutional unobservability” as a self-executing legal status.</p> <h2>15.4 Bypass Charter</h2> <p>A bypass may be created only by an authority competent over the affected function, with the standing of affected parties.</p> <p>Every charter shall specify:</p> <ul><li>problem and evidence;</li> <li>constituting authority;</li> <li>jurisdiction;</li> <li>participants and non-participants;</li> <li>rights floor;</li> <li>Indigenous and territorial interfaces;</li> <li>funding;</li> <li>data;</li> <li>experiment design;</li> <li>independent evaluator;</li> <li>sunset;</li> <li>challenge;</li> <li>terminal states.</li></ul> <p>Valid terminal states include:</p> <ul><li>integration into an existing institution;</li> <li>federation;</li> <li>stable coexistence;</li> <li>succession through lawful transfer;</li> <li>dissolution;</li> <li>termination for harm or failure.</li></ul> <p>Permanent polycentric coexistence is not inherently fragmentation.</p> <h2>15.5 Reintegration pressure</h2> <p>A bypass may publish evidence and offer integration. It may not automatically divert funding, seize authority, or impose escalating pressure on a core institution solely because the core declined to adopt its design.</p> <p>Any funding or authority change requires the ordinary constitutional process governing those resources or powers.</p> <h2>15.6 Functional transfer</h2> <p>Transfer of a public function requires:</p> <ul><li>lawful authority;</li> <li>service and rights continuity;</li> <li>affected-party standing;</li> <li>independent evaluation;</li> <li>worker and record transition;</li> <li>financial settlement;</li> <li>challenge and appeal;</li> <li>review after transfer.</li></ul> <p>Performance superiority is evidence relevant to the decision, not a source of jurisdiction.</p> <hr> <h1>16. Institutional succession and dissolution</h1> <h2>16.1 Dissolution triggers as alerts</h2> <p>Possible triggers include:</p> <ul><li>mission completion;</li> <li>persistent rights violation;</li> <li>capture;</li> <li>inability to perform the lawful mandate;</li> <li>loss of valid constituting authority;</li> <li>obsolete function;</li> <li>community or party decision under the charter.</li></ul> <p>Metrics and audits identify cases for review. They do not dissolve institutions automatically.</p> <h2>16.1.1 Function type and non-performing value</h2> <p><strong>Function type.</strong> Each institution shall be recorded as maintaining either a finite task or a standing condition.</p> <p>A finite-task institution states a completion criterion at constitution. It may not expand its remit, extend its timelines, or redefine its mandate where the effect is to defer that criterion; any extension requires independent authorization with published justification addressing this risk.</p> <p>A standing-condition institution carries no sunset clause. Where it can no longer maintain its obligation, its terminal act is transfer of that obligation to a body that can, not lapse.</p> <p><strong>Success as absence.</strong> Where an institution’s effective performance appears as the absence of an event, the absence of that event is not evidence that its function has become obsolete. Dissolution, merger, or material defunding of such an institution requires an affirmative demonstration that the underlying condition no longer obtains. Reductions may not be justified by favourable readings on indicators the institution itself produces.</p> <p><strong>Non-performing value.</strong> Before dissolution, merger, or material defunding, the review under §16.2 shall separately assess:</p> <ul><li><strong>warning value</strong> — whether the institution detects conditions that other institutions do not, independently of how well it performs its own mandate;</li> <li><strong>connective value</strong> — whether the institution maintains mutual legibility between other institutions that would not otherwise translate to each other.</li></ul> <p>Both contributions are invisible to assessment of mandate performance. An institution may perform its stated mandate poorly and still be load-bearing on either axis, and the loss registers only after the function is gone.</p> <h2>16.2 Independent authorization</h2> <p>The body proposing or benefiting from dissolution shall not be the sole authorizer.</p> <p>The process requires:</p> <ul><li>notice;</li> <li>statement of authority;</li> <li>evidence and State Estimate Record;</li> <li>affected-party participation;</li> <li>Indigenous standing where relevant;</li> <li>response;</li> <li>independent decision;</li> <li>appeal for rights, jurisdiction, or serious procedure;</li> <li>transition plan.</li></ul> <h2>16.3 Continuity plan</h2> <p>Before dissolution, specify:</p> <ul><li>essential services;</li> <li>outstanding obligations;</li> <li>personal and institutional records;</li> <li>worker status;</li> <li>public assets;</li> <li>ongoing cases;</li> <li>data custody;</li> <li>knowledge archives;</li> <li>successor or devolution;</li> <li>restoration if the decision is reversed.</li></ul> <h2>16.4 Chamber dissolution</h2> <p>Deadlock alone shall not automatically dissolve every participating chamber.</p> <p>Dissolution may be justified where a chamber persistently lacks valid composition, violates rights, exceeds jurisdiction, or cannot perform its mandate. The remedy shall target the defective institution rather than treating disagreement itself as illegitimacy.</p> <h2>16.5 Protocol preservation</h2> <p>Useful protocols may survive institutional dissolution. Preservation does not authorize automatic re-instantiation.</p> <p>A future body must receive its own valid mandate.</p> <hr> <h1>17. Capture resistance and accountability</h1> <h2>17.1 Structural tests</h2> <p>Institutions should be audited for:</p> <ul><li>self-justifying authority;</li> <li>asymmetric transparency;</li> <li>prohibitive exit costs;</li> <li>self-perpetuation as primary function;</li> <li>jurisdictional expansion;</li> <li>observer-beneficiary conflict;</li> <li>concentration of appointment and funding;</li> <li>inability to correct records;</li> <li>retaliation against refusal;</li> <li>control over essential resources;</li> <li>budget, promotion, appointment, and removal incentives;</li> <li>professional or organizational benefit from continuation;</li> <li>closure costs borne by staff or dependent communities;</li> <li>incentives of auditors, facilitators, funders, and vendors;</li> <li>conditions likely to reproduce the same behaviour after reform.</li></ul> <h2>17.1.1 Objective-reproduction review</h2> <p>Long-lived, systemically central, or self-renewing institutions and support ecosystems shall disclose the selection pressures likely to shape their operative behaviour, including:</p> <ul><li>how budgets expand or contract;</li> <li>how leaders and staff are appointed, promoted, disciplined, or removed;</li> <li>which failures produce blame and which remain diffuse;</li> <li>who benefits from mandate continuation or expansion;</li> <li>who bears the costs of closure, devolution, or simplification;</li> <li>whether the proposed reform changes these pressures;</li> <li>what would cause the previous behaviour to regenerate.</li></ul> <p>This review concerns observable institutional conditions, not presumed private motives.</p> <h2>17.2 No target veto frequency</h2> <p>A healthy system is not one in which every chamber vetoes 15–25 percent of decisions or makes a fixed share of critical decisions.</p> <p>Low veto use may reflect trust and good design. High use may reflect genuine threat or institutional dysfunction. Frequency is contextual evidence, not a health target.</p> <h2>17.3 No generalized reputation enforcement</h2> <p>The constitutional stack shall not maintain universal BAZ, person, council, or community trust scores that automatically alter:</p> <ul><li>exchange rates;</li> <li>trade terms;</li> <li>public standing;</li> <li>access to funds;</li> <li>treaty rights.</li></ul> <p>Specific defaults or breaches may produce proportionate contractual consequences through due process. They shall not become portable moral reputation.</p> <p>Recognition records are additionally governed by the purpose firewall in §10.4. Care, ecological, civic, cultural, relational, and analogous contribution records shall not be converted into portable moral, financial, political, or compliance reputation merely because they are available or predictive.</p> <h2>17.4 Independent challenge routes</h2> <p>Every consequential institution shall provide at least one challenge route outside the original decision chain.</p> <p>Depending on the domain, this may include:</p> <ul><li>ombuds;</li> <li>Tribunal;</li> <li>affected-community review;</li> <li>Indigenous authority;</li> <li>worker advocate;</li> <li>Office of the Adversary;</li> <li>independent audit assembly;</li> <li>judicial review.</li></ul> <h2>17.5 Refusal without retaliation</h2> <p>Conscientious refusers, whistleblowers, dissenting modelers, affected communities, and non-participants shall not lose unrelated rights or benefits merely because they challenge the process.</p> <p>Protection does not immunize unlawful harm. It prevents disagreement from being treated as capture or disloyalty by default.</p> <hr> <h1>18. Framework authority matrix</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>Framework or institution</th><th>Primary constitutional role</th><th>May define</th><th>Must defer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Treaty for Our Only Home</td><td>Constituted planetary and intergovernmental authority</td><td>Parties, powers, institutions, jurisdiction, law, finance, emergency authority</td><td>Indigenous internal legitimacy; domain mechanics; Meta-Governance self-expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing</td><td>Invitational container and statement of GGF obligations</td><td>Red Lines, non-interference, consent interfaces, provisional co-development proposals</td><td>Indigenous validation and internal law to Indigenous peoples themselves</td></tr><tr><td>Polycentric Governance Architecture</td><td>Horizontal separation of powers</td><td>Territorial, commons, practice, and sovereign interface topology; cross-boundary institutional forms</td><td>Currency, worker standards, Indigenous selection, enforcement law</td></tr><tr><td>Integrated Meta-Governance</td><td>Coordination among valid authorities</td><td>Interface protocols, convening, mediation, advisory observability and learning tools</td><td>Substantive jurisdiction, automatic succession, unilateral dissolution</td></tr><tr><td>GMEAIA</td><td>Shared method, authority-effect, lifecycle, handoff, correction, and closure grammar</td><td>Controlled records, states, response and custody semantics, consequence profiles, compatibility and migration rules</td><td>Substantive jurisdiction, causal truth, public authority, enforcement, legitimacy certification</td></tr><tr><td>Institutional Regeneration</td><td>Advisory mandate review, bounded internal reform support, lawful transition, and responsible closure</td><td>Purpose and mandate audits, reform options, transition and continuity records</td><td>Charter amendment, employment action, programme closure, dissolution, or successor authority without competent decision</td></tr><tr><td>Implementation & Adaptation</td><td>Context assessment, tool selection, bounded pilots, implementation support, learning, and transition</td><td>Support pathways, proportional implementation profiles, local adaptation, pilot and scaling evidence</td><td>Authority to enact, activate, fund, command, or scale substantive action</td></tr><tr><td>AUBI</td><td>Material security and adaptive social protection</td><td>Layers, entitlements, LMCI-M/LMCI-A separation, individual epistemic rights</td><td>Financial settlement and general labour governance</td></tr><tr><td>Financial Systems</td><td>Hearts, Leaves, Treasury, ICTL, reserves, settlement</td><td>Financial controls, redemption, rate process, financial rights</td><td>Meaning of work, Indigenous legitimacy, Treaty jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>Nested Economies</td><td>Economic subsidiarity and cross-scale interfaces</td><td>BAZ compacts, solidarity, interface contracts, protected non-convertibility</td><td>Monetary mechanics, worker rights, Indigenous law</td></tr><tr><td>Work in Liberation</td><td>Voluntary organized contribution and worker governance</td><td>Provider charters, worker rights, matching, commissioning, work mobilisation</td><td>AUBI baseline, currency operations, Treaty enforcement</td></tr><tr><td>Tribunals and justice frameworks</td><td>Adjudication within valid jurisdiction</td><td>Procedure, rights remedies, evidence, lawful sanctions</td><td>Sacred-knowledge validity and Indigenous internal authority</td></tr><tr><td>Technology governance</td><td>Technical and data safeguards</td><td>Security, identity, platform, algorithm, cybersecurity rules</td><td>Constitutional jurisdiction and substantive consent rights</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological and planetary-health frameworks</td><td>Ecological science and rights implementation</td><td>Observations, ecological thresholds, restoration standards</td><td>Automatic constitutional consequence without authorization</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>18.1 Conflict rule</h2> <p>Where two frameworks claim the same decision, the issue shall be resolved by:</p> <ol><li>decision class;</li> <li>constituting authority;</li> <li>specificity of mandate;</li> <li>affected rights and Indigenous standing;</li> <li>minimum necessary authority;</li> <li>independent adjudication where unresolved.</li></ol> <p>A later version does not silently override a constitutional boundary merely because it is newer.</p> <hr> <h1>19. Conformance requirements</h1> <p>A framework conforms to this Specification only if it can answer the following questions for each consequential mechanism.</p> <ol><li>Who constituted the authority?</li> <li>Whom does it bind?</li> <li>What subject and territory does it govern?</li> <li>Which decision class applies?</li> <li>What observation supports action? <ul><li>What information class and constituting purpose applies to each material source?</li> <li>Where information is used beyond that purpose, what independent authority, necessity, less-intrusive-source analysis, minimum-use rule, provenance, and challenge route permit the secondary use?</li> <li>Where the decision depends on a valuation, what purpose was that valuation produced for, by whom, as of what date, under what uncertainty and liquidity assumption, and what reuse is prohibited?</li> <li>Where the observation depends on a voluntary act of disclosure, what does the discloser risk, is the intervention under evaluation expected to raise or lower disclosure, and what independent channel does not depend on the same act?</li></ul></li> <li>What alternative interpretations remain?</li> <li>Who proposes, authorizes, implements, adjudicates, and evaluates?</li> <li>What Indigenous authorities and rights are affected?</li> <li>What may be refused, adapted, vetoed, or withheld?</li> <li>What may happen automatically?</li> <li>What rights and material systems remain protected?</li> <li>What is the appeal route?</li> <li>What is the sunset or return condition?</li> <li>What independent outcome would falsify the intervention?</li> <li>What happens if the observation or decision was wrong?</li> <li>Who controls the principal causal variables, legally and practically?</li> <li>What response clocks, refusal states, and escalation routes apply?</li> <li>What common-mode dependencies could disable or bias several channels at once?</li> <li>Is the documentation burden proportionate to the action’s consequences?</li> <li>What evidence would justify narrowing, suspending, replacing, or declining to scale the framework or mechanism itself?</li> <li>Where person identity or continuity matters, what purpose-limited identifier or attestation is actually necessary; what authority permits any cross-domain recognition; how are non-transitivity, collision, correction, external status changes, and surviving duties handled?</li> <li>For each assumed operating condition recorded under §2.15 and Appendix L.2, which external function, institution, infrastructure, or state variable must hold for that condition to obtain, and is it named anywhere in this framework? <ul><li>For each, is it allocated to an identified owner, disclaimed as out of scope, or not mentioned at all?</li> <li>For each, does this framework’s own machinery, operating as designed, amplify its effect or convert an outcome of this framework into an input to it?</li> <li>Where a function is unnamed and endogenously amplified, state whether it is a deliberate non-goal, a seam requiring an interface, an observation gap, or a capability no identified actor is required or able to supply.</li></ul></li></ol> <p>Questions 1–19 and 21 apply to consequential mechanisms as relevant. Question 20 applies at framework level and to long-lived, systemically central, or self-renewing mechanisms; it is not a required field for every local action.</p> <p>Question 22 applies at framework level and at each substantial revision. It is answered by enumerating what the framework does not say. A framework that answers it by listing only functions it already mentions, or by restating its Appendix L.2 conditions without identifying what each condition depends on, has not answered it. An external dependency that the framework neither amplifies nor converts into an input to itself may be recorded and left there; the question does not require a framework to acquire authority over anything it names.</p> <h2>19.1 Non-conformance categories</h2> <ul><li><strong>P0 Constitutional conflict:</strong> mechanism claims authority without a valid source, overrides Indigenous sovereignty, or creates irreversible consequence from an indicator.</li> <li><strong>P1 Interface gap:</strong> authority is plausible but records, purpose-limited data use, person-continuity safeguards where relevant, challenge, continuity, or decision separation are missing.</li> <li><strong>P2 Operational gap:</strong> implementation, metrics, or technical details remain under-specified without immediate constitutional contradiction.</li> <li><strong>P3 Dependency gap:</strong> an external function materially determines the framework’s outcomes and is amplified by its operation, but is neither allocated to an identified owner nor disclaimed as out of scope. No interface is malformed, because none is represented.</li></ul> <h2>19.2 Required revision record</h2> <p>Every revised framework shall publish:</p> <ul><li>specification version used;</li> <li>provisions adopted;</li> <li>intentional divergences;</li> <li>unresolved dependencies;</li> <li>compatibility tests performed.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>20. Implementation sequence</h1> <h2>20.1 Immediate migration sequence</h2> <p>The v0.2.1 migration sequence remains the baseline for the shared method and audited framework stack:</p> <ol><li>GMEAIA v0.2.1;</li> <li>Integrated Meta-Governance;</li> <li>Nested Economies;</li> <li>Institutional Regeneration;</li> <li>Implementation & Adaptation.</li></ol> <h2>20.2 Broader constitutional consistency sequence</h2> <p>After the immediate migration, conduct a broader consistency pass through:</p> <ul><li>Treaty for Our Only Home;</li> <li>Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing;</li> <li>Polycentric Governance Architecture;</li> <li>AUBI, Financial Systems, and Work in Liberation;</li> <li>justice, technology, disability, peace, ecological, and crisis frameworks.</li></ul> <h2>20.3 Indigenous-led gate</h2> <p>Before Indigenous-specific institutional proposals are treated as implementation-ready, the GGF should seek Indigenous-led review through relationships and processes determined by participating peoples.</p> <p>Absence of such review shall be visible in status labels. It shall not be compensated for by additional AI review.</p> <h2>20.4 Legal gate</h2> <p>Treaty enforcement, universal jurisdiction, taxation, emergency deployment, BAZ conversion, Commons Trust veto, and chamber dissolution require dedicated legal analysis in the relevant jurisdictions.</p> <p>Conceptual coherence is not legal validity.</p> <h2>20.5 Empirical and simulation gate</h2> <p>The following require stress testing before scale:</p> <ul><li>cross-boundary Commons Trusts;</li> <li>multi-jurisdiction deadlock;</li> <li>bypass coexistence and succession;</li> <li>emergency authority;</li> <li>institutional dissolution;</li> <li>economic continuity after charter revocation;</li> <li>observer dependence and capture detection;</li> <li>small-community role collapsing;</li> <li>response-clock and omission-accountability design;</li> <li>causal-responsibility mapping;</li> <li>common-mode observer and infrastructure failure;</li> <li>consequence-scaled documentation profiles.</li></ul> <h2>20.6 Stack consistency pass</h2> <p>After the five immediate migrations, conduct one shared consistency pass across the revised stack and its dependencies. The pass shall test authority formation, response states, causal responsibility, proportionality, protected domains, correction propagation, closure, and version compatibility.</p> <h2>20.7 Recognition-record compatibility and migration</h2> <p>Version 0.2.2 introduced an additive and restrictive constitutional patch. Version 0.2.3 is its controlled corrective successor and does not alter the authority, jurisdiction, consequence-profile, response-state, clock, or lifecycle semantics against which frameworks were migrated to v0.2.1 or the recognition-record doctrine introduced in v0.2.2.</p> <p>Accordingly:</p> <ul><li>a framework explicitly pinned to <code>CIS/0.2.1</code> or <code>CIS/0.2.2</code> remains governed by and assessable against that declared compatibility target until it is substantively revised or expressly adopts v0.2.3;</li> <li>publication of v0.2.3 does not by itself require a hash-only repin of already migrated frameworks;</li> <li>new frameworks and substantive revisions should target v0.2.3 unless they identify and justify a different compatible version;</li> <li>a framework that claims conformance to v0.2.3 shall satisfy §10.4 and the corresponding Observation Contract and conformance requirements.</li></ul> <p>The next substantive revision of frameworks that create, consume, transform, or infer from recognition records should check at least:</p> <ul><li>AUBI;</li> <li>Financial Systems;</li> <li>Nested Economies;</li> <li>Work in Liberation;</li> <li>Polycentric Governance Architecture;</li> <li>Technology and data-governance frameworks;</li> <li>any framework using Love Ledger, contribution evidence, care or ecological recognition, participant reputation, or derived behavioural signals.</li></ul> <p>The migration review shall identify:</p> <ol><li>recognition records currently created or consumed;</li> <li>their Class C, Class D, or protected Class E status;</li> <li>any existing consequential secondary uses;</li> <li>whether a purpose-appropriate financial or operational record is created when recognized activity generates an asset, payment, entitlement, liability, or transaction;</li> <li>uses that may continue under an independent authority and §10.4.1;</li> <li>uses that should migrate to purpose-appropriate records or bounded attestations;</li> <li>uses that should stop;</li> <li>historical records requiring retention, correction, restricted access, or deletion under applicable law and governance.</li></ol> <p>Adoption of v0.2.2 or v0.2.3 does not automatically invalidate historical records or completed decisions. Where an existing use would be non-conforming under §10.4, the responsible framework shall prevent unnecessary expansion of the use, preserve correction and appeal rights, and establish a proportionate migration plan through §19.2 rather than silently continuing or retroactively rewriting the record.</p> <h2>20.8 Person-continuity compatibility and migration</h2> <p>Version 0.2.4 is additive and restrictive. It does not create new civil-registration, citizenship, family-law, migration-status, electoral, benefit, adjudicative, or universal-identity authority.</p> <p>Accordingly:</p> <ul><li>frameworks explicitly pinned to earlier compatible CIS versions remain governed by those declared targets until substantively revised or until they expressly adopt v0.2.4;</li> <li>new frameworks and substantive revisions that create or consume purpose-limited person identifiers, cross-domain continuity assertions, or external civil-status transitions should target v0.2.4 unless they identify and justify another compatible rule;</li> <li>no framework is required to replace a lawful local identifier merely because v0.2.4 exists;</li> <li>adoption of v0.2.4 does not authorize retrospective construction of a master identity graph from historical records;</li> <li>existing pairwise links shall not be treated as transitively authorizing new links.</li></ul> <p>The first conformance pass should examine at least:</p> <ul><li>AUBI access attestations and any Love Ledger-derived identifier language;</li> <li>Housing provisional/local claimant identifiers and succession/status bridges;</li> <li>Migration self-, community-, family-, witness-, and service-attestation routes;</li> <li>Kinship purpose-limited identifiers and community attestations;</li> <li>Aurora identity separation, anti-linkage, and civil-status ingestion rules;</li> <li>Shared Capital beneficiary succession;</li> <li>Adaptive Tax inheritance and taxpayer-continuity interfaces;</li> <li>Hearthstone bequest, succession, claimant, and unresolved-duty records;</li> <li>Justice standing processes where claimant continuity is operationally necessary.</li></ul> <p>The migration review shall identify:</p> <ol><li>each purpose-limited identifier or attestation;</li> <li>its issuer, authority, purpose, scope, relying parties, prohibited uses, expiry, correction, and challenge;</li> <li>any existing cross-domain links;</li> <li>any transitive or universal-correlation assumptions that must be removed;</li> <li>mistaken-merge, mistaken-split, duplicate, collision, and correction handling;</li> <li>any external status events consumed by the framework;</li> <li>how death or another external status change affects active participation without erasing unresolved duties, claims, remedies, or succession questions;</li> <li>any case in which essential access currently depends unnecessarily on general identity;</li> <li>any case in which a Love Ledger, reputation, contribution, behavioural, or recognition record is being promoted into identity or eligibility infrastructure;</li> <li>any function that remains with competent external civil, family, citizenship, migration, electoral, succession, or judicial authority.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>Appendix A — Plural Legitimacy Record</h1> <p><strong>Institution or power:</strong><br> <strong>Constituting authority:</strong><br> <strong>Participants or parties:</strong><br> <strong>Persons or territories bound:</strong><br> <strong>Non-member effects:</strong><br> <strong>Subject-matter jurisdiction:</strong><br> <strong>Territorial jurisdiction:</strong><br> <strong>Indigenous peoples and authorities affected:</strong><br> <strong>Rights protected:</strong><br> <strong>Funding source:</strong><br> <strong>Decision rule:</strong><br> <strong>Consent, refusal, veto, and exit:</strong><br> <strong>Adjudication:</strong><br> <strong>Sunset or withdrawal:</strong><br> <strong>Continuity duties:</strong><br> <strong>Unresolved legitimacy questions:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix B — Causal Responsibility and Constitutional Authority Profile</h1> <p><strong>Decision or condition:</strong><br> <strong>Decision class:</strong><br> <strong>Current authority:</strong><br> <strong>Proposed or shared authority:</strong><br> <strong>Legal or constitutional source:</strong><br> <strong>Principal causal variables:</strong><br> <strong>Legal controller of each variable:</strong><br> <strong>Practical controller of each variable:</strong><br> <strong>Affected parties and non-members:</strong><br> <strong>Burdens assigned:</strong><br> <strong>Resources and powers transferred:</strong><br> <strong>Upstream and external dependencies:</strong><br> <strong>Continuing duties of actors retaining control:</strong><br> <strong>Condition requiring action:</strong><br> <strong>Why lower-scale, voluntary, or narrower action is insufficient:</strong><br> <strong>Support offered before transfer:</strong><br> <strong>Least authority required:</strong><br> <strong>Local discretion preserved:</strong><br> <strong>Protected rights and material minimums:</strong><br> <strong>Impossible mandate or pilot condition:</strong><br> <strong>Constitutional gap:</strong><br> <strong>Duration:</strong><br> <strong>Challenge and appeal:</strong><br> <strong>Independent outcomes:</strong><br> <strong>Return or devolution condition:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix C — Observation Contract</h1> <p><strong>Decision and class:</strong><br> <strong>Latent condition:</strong><br> <strong>Observations used:</strong><br> <strong>Observations unavailable:</strong><br> <strong>Sources and dependencies:</strong><br> <strong>Information class and constituting purpose of each material source:</strong><br> <strong>Primary, compatible-secondary, or consequential-secondary use:</strong><br> <strong>Recognition-record secondary-use authority, necessity, and less-intrusive-source analysis, if applicable:</strong><br> <strong>Material transformations into financial, eligibility, risk, compliance, or enforcement inference:</strong><br> <strong>Population coverage:</strong><br> <strong>Missing-population risk:</strong><br> <strong>Disclosure dependence — required act and discloser:</strong><br> <strong>Disclosure risk borne by the discloser:</strong><br> <strong>Expected effect of the intervention on disclosure:</strong><br> <strong>Independent channel not dependent on the same disclosure act, or statement that none exists:</strong> <strong>Protected knowledge boundaries:</strong><br> <strong>Time scale and lag:</strong><br> <strong>Model or interpretive method:</strong><br> <strong>Known uncertainty:</strong><br> <strong>Alternative interpretations:</strong><br> <strong>Sensor version:</strong><br> <strong>Independent outcome measures:</strong><br> <strong>Correction and challenge route:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix D — State Estimate Record</h1> <p><strong>Leading estimate:</strong><br> <strong>Coexisting conditions:</strong><br> <strong>Plausible alternatives:</strong><br> <strong>Incompatible explanations:</strong><br> <strong>Evidence supporting each:</strong><br> <strong>Evidence against each:</strong><br> <strong>Confidence and uncertainty:</strong><br> <strong>Source-independence concerns:</strong><br> <strong>Cost of wrong action:</strong><br> <strong>Cause resolved, plural, conflicting, or unresolved:</strong><br> <strong>Recommended containment:</strong><br> <strong>Authorization required:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix E — Polycentric Interface Record</h1> <p><strong>Initiating authority:</strong><br> <strong>Affected territorial authority:</strong><br> <strong>Affected commons authority:</strong><br> <strong>Affected practice or labour authority:</strong><br> <strong>Affected Indigenous authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Rights and Tribunal interface:</strong><br> <strong>Cross-domain effects:</strong><br> <strong>Consent or veto required:</strong><br> <strong>Information exchanged:</strong><br> <strong>Protected non-transfer:</strong><br> <strong>Dispute process:</strong><br> <strong>Outcome and review:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix F — Emergency Constitutionalism Record</h1> <p><strong>Declaration and legal basis:</strong><br> <strong>Crisis condition:</strong><br> <strong>Decision class:</strong><br> <strong>Evidence and uncertainty:</strong><br> <strong>Provisional action:</strong><br> <strong>Authorized body:</strong><br> <strong>Geographic and subject scope:</strong><br> <strong>Powers activated:</strong><br> <strong>Powers prohibited:</strong><br> <strong>Local and Indigenous interface:</strong><br> <strong>Protected material minimums:</strong><br> <strong>Work mobilisation:</strong><br> <strong>Duration and renewal:</strong><br> <strong>Oversight:</strong><br> <strong>Termination:</strong><br> <strong>Restoration and restitution:</strong><br> <strong>After-action review:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix G — Bypass Charter</h1> <p><strong>Problem and evidence:</strong><br> <strong>Constituting authority:</strong><br> <strong>Jurisdiction:</strong><br> <strong>Participants and non-participants:</strong><br> <strong>Affected rights and Indigenous authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Rights floor:</strong><br> <strong>Funding and assets:</strong><br> <strong>Observation and data:</strong><br> <strong>Experiment design:</strong><br> <strong>Independent evaluator:</strong><br> <strong>Challenge route:</strong><br> <strong>Sunset:</strong><br> <strong>Terminal states:</strong> integration / federation / coexistence / lawful succession / dissolution / termination<br> <strong>Continuity plan:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix H — Revocation, Transfer, or Dissolution Continuity Plan</h1> <p><strong>Institution or charter affected:</strong><br> <strong>Authority and grounds:</strong><br> <strong>Harm to stop:</strong><br> <strong>Narrow containment already attempted:</strong><br> <strong>Essential services:</strong><br> <strong>AUBI and personal balances:</strong><br> <strong>Workers and participants:</strong><br> <strong>Public and community assets:</strong><br> <strong>Records and active claims:</strong><br> <strong>Data and protected knowledge:</strong><br> <strong>Successor, devolution, or closure:</strong><br> <strong>Appeal:</strong><br> <strong>Restoration if reversed:</strong><br> <strong>Independent outcome review:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix I — Polycentric Framework Integration Note</h1> <p>The Polycentric Governance Architecture Framework, in the edition from which this Specification was drawn, contributes the following elements:</p> <ul><li>overlapping functional jurisdictions;</li> <li>territorial, commons, and practice/labour separation;</li> <li>cross-boundary Commons Trusts;</li> <li>Guardian roles for non-human interests;</li> <li>fractal institutional scaling;</li> <li>the principle that Meta-Governance coordinates rather than overrides;</li> <li>recognition that a BAZ should not monopolize all authority within its borders.</li></ul> <p>The following elements of that edition required revision before constitutional adoption, and have been addressed in the current Polycentric framework:</p> <ul><li>the claim that tyranny or capture becomes logistically impossible;</li> <li>a GGF-designed universal Indigenous Wisdom Council, including externally specified nomination, sortition, quotas, or veto limits;</li> <li>automatic chamber dissolution and temporary jury government after deadlock;</li> <li>composite Polycentric Health Index controls;</li> <li>target veto frequencies and fixed decision shares;</li> <li>trust and reputation scores that alter trade or exchange rates;</li> <li>automatic Treaty enforcement from dashboard thresholds;</li> <li>universal public blockchain logging;</li> <li>Love Ledger sentiment measurement;</li> <li>guild authority over Hearts rates beyond Work in Liberation and Financial Systems mandates;</li> <li>resource-flow restrictions or economic isolation without rights-bounded adjudication;</li> <li>automatic financial penalties for alleged capture.</li></ul> <p>The constitutional synthesis is therefore:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Overlapping jurisdictions should make capture harder, visible, and contestable. They do not eliminate the need for due process, independent observation, protected continuity, legitimate Indigenous authority, or limits on every corrective institution.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>Appendix J — Response, Refusal, and Omission Constitutional Minimum</h1> <p>This appendix states the minimum constitutional information that must remain representable. Exact field names, controlled states, schemas, clock rules, and lifecycle transitions are governed by the current compatible GMEAIA release.</p> <p><strong>Source request or finding:</strong><br> <strong>Candidate duty-holder and basis for competence:</strong><br> <strong>Response-duty source and effect:</strong><br> <strong>Acknowledgment- and decision-clock sources:</strong><br> <strong>Response and reasons:</strong><br> <strong>Conflicts and missing prerequisites:</strong><br> <strong>Expected harm during delay:</strong><br> <strong>Provisional safe-state authority, if any:</strong><br> <strong>Sender duties retained:</strong><br> <strong>Recipient duties accepted:</strong><br> <strong>Capacity limitation or jurisdictional absence:</strong><br> <strong>Escalation and review:</strong><br> <strong>Unresolved-duty custody:</strong><br> <strong>Closure condition:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix K — Independence and Objective-Reproduction Constitutional Minimum</h1> <p>Independence review and objective-reproduction review are separately triggerable. A mechanism may require either or both. Exact operational fields and controlled states are governed by GMEAIA.</p> <h2>K.1 Independence</h2> <p><strong>Shared observers and personnel:</strong><br> <strong>Shared data and databases:</strong><br> <strong>Shared AI models and providers:</strong><br> <strong>Shared platforms, vendors, and infrastructure:</strong><br> <strong>Shared funders and appointment authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Shared methods, ontologies, and professional networks:</strong><br> <strong>Common points of failure:</strong><br> <strong>Fallback and independent channels:</strong><br> <strong>Residual correlation risk and accepting authority:</strong></p> <h2>K.2 Objective reproduction</h2> <p><strong>Budget and revenue incentives:</strong><br> <strong>Appointment, promotion, discipline, and removal incentives:</strong><br> <strong>Prestige and blame exposure:</strong><br> <strong>Benefits of continuation or expansion:</strong><br> <strong>Costs of closure, devolution, or simplification:</strong><br> <strong>Incentives of auditors, facilitators, funders, and vendors:</strong><br> <strong>Behaviour likely to regenerate after reform:</strong><br> <strong>Selection pressures changed by the proposal:</strong><br> <strong>Conditions making the mechanism unnecessary:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix L — Proportionality and Falsification Constitutional Minimum</h1> <p>This appendix states constitutional requirements rather than an independent operational schema. GMEAIA governs consequence-profile codes and record bundles. Framework-level falsification applies to frameworks and long-lived, systemically central, or self-renewing mechanisms, not to every local action.</p> <h2>L.1 Proportionality</h2> <p><strong>Reversibility:</strong><br> <strong>Rights and service effects:</strong><br> <strong>Coercion or public-law effect:</strong><br> <strong>Non-participant exposure:</strong><br> <strong>Ecological irreversibility:</strong><br> <strong>Data sensitivity:</strong><br> <strong>Duration and scale:</strong><br> <strong>Required record depth:</strong><br> <strong>Existing records reused:</strong><br> <strong>Justified omissions:</strong><br> <strong>Administrative burden and distribution:</strong><br> <strong>Accessible and low-tech equivalent:</strong></p> <h2>L.2 Falsification</h2> <p><strong>Framework or long-lived mechanism:</strong><br> <strong>Theory of change:</strong><br> <strong>Expected causal mechanism:</strong><br> <strong>Disconfirming evidence:</strong><br> <strong>Architecture-added latency and burden:</strong><br> <strong>Narrowing condition:</strong><br> <strong>Suspension condition:</strong><br> <strong>Replacement or non-scaling condition:</strong><br> <strong>Review date and competent decision authority:</strong> <strong>Assumed operating conditions:</strong> <strong>Stated non-applicability:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix M — Person-Continuity and Attestation Constitutional Minimum</h1> <p>This appendix defines a minimum constitutional record, not a civil registry, universal identity schema, or new GMEAIA lifecycle. Implementations may embed these fields in existing records if the semantic functions remain explicit.</p> <p><strong>Record ID:</strong><br> <strong>Consequence profile / action context:</strong><br> <strong>Person-facing purpose:</strong><br> <strong>Source framework / system:</strong><br> <strong>Source local identifier or bounded reference:</strong><br> <strong>Target framework / system:</strong><br> <strong>Target local identifier or bounded reference:</strong><br> <strong>Continuity relation asserted:</strong> same person / distinct person / correction / unresolved<br> <strong>Assertion source:</strong> person / authorized representative / competent authority / bounded attester<br> <strong>Constituting authority or lawful basis:</strong><br> <strong>Necessity and less-intrusive alternative:</strong><br> <strong>Minimum disclosed attributes:</strong><br> <strong>Source evidence and uncertainty:</strong><br> <strong>Permitted relying parties:</strong><br> <strong>Prohibited uses:</strong><br> <strong>Non-transitivity notice:</strong><br> <strong>Affected-person notice status:</strong><br> <strong>Correction route:</strong><br> <strong>Mistaken-merge route:</strong><br> <strong>Mistaken-split route:</strong><br> <strong>Duplicate/collision status:</strong><br> <strong>Challenge / review route:</strong><br> <strong>Expiry / unlink / renewed-authorization condition:</strong><br> <strong>External status event, if any:</strong><br> <strong>External status source authority and effective date:</strong><br> <strong>Domain-local consequence only:</strong><br> <strong>Surviving claims, liabilities, remedies, succession questions, and unresolved duties:</strong><br> <strong>GMEAIA handoff / response / closure references, if applicable:</strong><br> <strong>Aurora data-purpose, access, retention, and disposition references:</strong><br> <strong>Public summary or protected basis:</strong><br> <strong>Integrity / provenance reference:</strong></p> <h3>M.1 Conformance rules</h3> <p>A conforming record shall not:</p> <ul><li>create a universal identifier;</li> <li>replace a competent civil registry;</li> <li>infer citizenship, nationality, migration status, voting eligibility, family status, legal capacity, or inheritance entitlement beyond a separately valid source;</li> <li>treat pairwise continuity as transitive;</li> <li>expose unrelated source-domain data merely because same-person continuity was accepted;</li> <li>silently merge source records;</li> <li>erase the correction history of a mistaken merge or split;</li> <li>infer death from inactivity or non-participation;</li> <li>close unresolved duties solely because an external death/status event was received.</li></ul> <h3>M.2 Minimality rule</h3> <p>Where the receiving function can be satisfied by an attribute attestation such as “over threshold,” “authorized representative,” “same claimant as prior case,” or “external death record verified,” the system should not request or construct a more general identity record.</p> <hr> <h1>Appendix N — Valuation Statement Constitutional Minimum</h1> <p>This appendix defines a minimum constitutional record for any valuation on which a consequential decision depends. It does not establish a canonical valuation, a valuation authority, an accounting or reporting standard, or a rating function. Implementations may embed these fields in existing records if the semantic functions remain explicit.</p> <p><strong>Record ID:</strong><br> <strong>Object valued:</strong><br> <strong>Valuation purpose:</strong> fiscal base / prudential or liquidity / fiduciary permissibility / ecological outcome / compensation or restitution / accounting or reporting / other stated purpose<br> <strong>Consequential decision the valuation supports:</strong><br> <strong>Valuing authority and constituting basis:</strong><br> <strong>Independence of valuer from the decision:</strong><br> <strong>Method and its source:</strong><br> <strong>Inputs and their provenance:</strong><br> <strong>As-of date and validity horizon:</strong><br> <strong>Liquidity assumption:</strong> immediate / committed facility / haircut-adjusted / illiquid / not applicable<br> <strong>Market-observability status:</strong> observed price / comparable / modelled / non-market<br> <strong>Uncertainty, range, and known sensitivity:</strong><br> <strong>Alternative methods considered and why not used:</strong><br> <strong>Known divergence from other valuations of the same object, and their purposes:</strong><br> <strong>Prohibited reuse:</strong><br> <strong>Affected-party notice status:</strong><br> <strong>Challenge and revaluation route:</strong><br> <strong>Correction and propagation route:</strong><br> <strong>Superseded by / supersedes:</strong></p> <h3>N.1 Conformance rules</h3> <p>A conforming valuation shall not:</p> <ul><li>be reused for a purpose other than the one stated without separate authority and a new record;</li> <li>be treated as the value of the object rather than the value for a stated purpose;</li> <li>convert a care, ecological, cultural, sacred, or relational recognition into a market price, or a market price into such a recognition, where a protected non-convertibility applies;</li> <li>present a modelled or non-market valuation as an observed price;</li> <li>omit uncertainty on the ground that a decision requires a single figure;</li> <li>be adopted from an external rating, index, model, or vendor without recording that source, its method status, and its conflicts;</li> <li>silently supersede a prior valuation whose consequences remain live.</li></ul> <h3>N.2 Divergence rule</h3> <p>Two conforming valuations of the same object for different purposes may differ, and the difference is not by itself an error, a finding of misvaluation, or evidence that one valuer is wrong.</p> <p>Where divergence is material to a decision, both records and their stated purposes shall be visible to the deciding party rather than reconciled into a single figure. No framework acquires authority over another framework’s valuation by recording a divergence from it.</p> <h3>N.3 Proportionality</h3> <p>This record applies to valuations supporting consequential decisions. Routine, low-consequence, or internally reversible valuations require no separate record. Appendix L applies.</p> <hr> <h1>Conclusion</h1> <p>The constitutional stack should not be one pyramid with the Treaty at the top, Meta-Governance as its nervous system, and Indigenous authority as an integrated legitimacy module.</p> <p>It should be a network of differently constituted authorities connected through explicit interfaces.</p> <p>The Treaty may bind its parties within conferred jurisdiction. Indigenous peoples remain sovereign beyond and within those arrangements according to their own authority. Territorial institutions govern place. Commons institutions protect systems that cross boundaries. Labour and practice institutions govern how work is organized. Tribunals adjudicate valid law. Meta-Governance helps these institutions see and coordinate with one another without becoming their sovereign.</p> <p>The governing rule is:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>No observation creates jurisdiction, no coordination function creates sovereignty, no representation substitutes for consent, and no corrective mechanism is exempt from the constitutional limits it applies to others.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>The operational rule is:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>For every consequential act, identify who constituted the authority, what condition is believed to exist, who controls its principal causes, what uncertainty remains, who must respond and by when, who may refuse or veto, what may happen automatically, what remains protected, what person-continuity assumptions are actually necessary, who adjudicates, and how power returns when the justification ends.</strong></p></blockquote>",1);function c(e){var i=r();n(1474),o(e,i)}export{c as default};
